\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar mixed in Europe, little affected by Clinton. \b0\par\par\uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 11:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 18 (Reuters) - The dollar was firm against the mark in European morning trade on Tuesday, but was near its overnight lows against the yen amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The market was relieved that there had been no surprises from U.S. President Bill Clinton's testimony on Monday about his relationship with former White House intern Monica Lewinsky, analysts said, but added that the dollar did not seem to be much affected by the issue. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 1.7996/01 marks at 0921 GMT after trading in a range of less than one pfennig overnight. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was at 1.7914/19 in late Europe Monday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would now move on to look elsewhere for incentive after Clinton said he had a relationship with Lewinsky which was "not appropriate". \par \par \uc2  "Clinton looks like he's in safer waters now, after his reasonably candid admission of what happened," said Michael Derks, senior market strategist at Nomura International. \par \par \uc2  "What's going on in Russia will continue be something that the market will be looking at very closely, and Japan is also going to be a key factor," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark rose to a five-week high on Monday after Russia effectively devalued the rouble, although it had given up most of those gains by the end of the day. \par \par \uc2  "So far Russia seems quieter than one might have expected, but it's not necessarily over yet. We'll get a lot of details about what they're going to do next," said Francis Breedon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Lehman Brothers in London. \par \par \uc2  Breedon said the market would be looking at the trade figures for Germany and the United States for evidence of how hard they are being hit by the Asian crisis. \par \par \uc2  Germany's June trade surplus, announced on Tuesday, was 11.2 billion marks compared with 14.2 billion in May. The U.S. trade balance for June, due at 1230 GMT, is forecast at a deficit of $15.4 billion after a record deficit of $15.75 billion in May. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen bulls, meanwhile, were holding off after Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said overnight that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was always ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. Vice Finance Minister Eisuke Sakakibara said he believed it was the market's perception that the yen had hit bottom at 147 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was quoted at 145.70/80, almost unchanged from levels seen late on Monday and down 1.3 percent from the eight-year high of 147.63 seen last week. \par \par \uc2  "In general, dollar/yen has not performed as well as might have been expected, given everything that's going on. In part, it's due to some weakness in mark/yen," said Derks at Nomura. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen has dropped more than two percent from the near-six-year high of 82.80 seen last week, a key factor for the drop being the mark's weakness across the board on worries about the Russian financial situation. \par \par \uc2  The British pound was firmer against the mark, cheered up briefly by British data which showed retail price inflation did not drop as much as expected in July. \par \par \uc2  Headline RPI fell by 0.2 percent on the month against expectations of a 0.3 percent decrease. But the closely watched core inflation RPIX came out in line with expectations, growing by 2.6 percent on the year. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark was quoted at 2.9045/55, up 2.8990/95 late on Monday. Sterling/dollar was at $1.6150/60 against $1.6172/82 Monday. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i010vc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
