\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS - Mark erases losses, but top-heavy. \b0\par\par\uc2 547 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 06:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 18 (Reuters) - The German mark erased earlier losses against the yen on short-covering by early afternoon in Tokyo cross trade on Tuesday, as the dollar also recouped some losses against yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the mark lacked upward momentum over lingering concerns about Russia's financial woes and about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen, they said. \par \par \uc2  "As the situation in Russia remains unstable, it's hard to buy up the mark," a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0421 GMT, the mark stood at 81.29/32 yen, compared with 81.27 yen in late New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Mark was hit by large bouts of profit-taking after initially rising to near 81.65 yen in early Asia, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Mark losses were spurred by dollar's drop against yen, they added. \par \par \uc2  "Mark was sold as dollar/yen hit stop-loss sales, but trade petered out after mark/yen found support at 81 yen," a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese importers and some investors were seen picking up the mark on dips, some dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, mark/yen's upside was stemmed by heightened concerns over possible yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a cascade of comments from Finance Ministry officials. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said at a news conference on Tuesday morning that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said on Tuesday he believed it was the market's perception that the yen had hit bottom at 147 yen to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Asked if the yen at hit bottom at 147 yen to the dollar, Sakakibara told reporters, "If you look at the charts, it hit bottom. This is not me, but the market." \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, deputy-general of the MOF's International Bureau, also said that an excessively weak yen was undesirable. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen's upside also faltered on a rebound in Tokyo stock prices. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei-225 stock average was up 1.47 percent or 217.38 points at 15,012.04 as of 0417 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dealers remained mixed over the direction of mark/yen. \par \par \uc2  Some saw the mark remaining firm due to Germany's economic recovery and the upcoming launch of the euro. \par \par \uc2  But others said Russia's financial woes would continue to weigh on the German \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite a recovery from knee-jerk mark-selling on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Russia on Monday declared a 90-day moratorium on repaying some foreign debt and an effective devaluation of the rouble. \par \par \uc2  Russia expanded the rouble corridor and restricted foreign investment in Russian rouble instruments. \par \par \uc2  The government-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement said it would keep the value of rouble in the range of six to 9-per dollar until the end of the year, compared with a little more than six to the dollar so far this year. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Swiss franc stood at 96.91/96 yen from around 96.84 yen in late New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at 235.60/72 yen compared with 235.49 yen in New York. Ecu was at 160.22/27 yen, slightly higher than earlier trade. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i0102x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
