\par\par \b \uc2  World Market Turmoil At A Glance:Fed Leaves Rates Unchanged \b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1998\par 00:07\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- The Federal Open Market Committee left short-term\par interest rates unchanged Tuesday\par    -- President Clinton did the political equivalent of\par betting the White House in Monday night's speech and the\par immediate reactions from Republicans show they are ready to\par match his wager and raise the ante\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- A day after President Clinton admitted to having an\par "inappropriate relationship" with her, Monica Lewinsky\par Tuesday was summoned back to give additional grand jury\par testimony Thursday in the aftermath of Clinton's account of\par their relationship, sources familiar with the request said.\par    -- Old leaders reasserted themselves, as a rally that\par got underway in technology, banking and retail sectors\par spread to the broad market and swept major market averages\par to sharp gains for the second straight session.\par    -- Panicky investors dumped dollars late Tuesday as\par speculation increased that the Japanese government would\par take measures to support its ailing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said.\par The dollar finished significantly lower against the yen, hit\par first by jawboning Japanese officials and later by\par soothsaying U.S. analysts, all predicting Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was in sight.\par    -- U.S. Treasurys were lower late Tuesday, as strength\par in worldwide equity markets, and a narrowing of the U.S.\par trade deficit sapped some of Treasurys' safe-haven bid.\par    -- After a sharp slump during the morning, Brady bonds\par are reversing their trend to close mostly stronger late\par Tuesday on short covering.\par    -- Russia's finance ministry and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Tuesday\par called for a meeting of leading local and foreign banks for\par talks over the rescheduling of Russian banks' foreign debt\par    -- The bond market continued to be fearful and uncertain\par following the second statement in two days from Russia's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par    -- German Economics Minister Guenter Rexrodt said a new\par round of international credits for Russia couldn't be ruled\par out, given the country's current financial situation.\par system=djm;\par    -- Large German banks remained adamant Tuesday that they\par aren't heavily exposed in Russia, after the Russian\par government clarified the 90-day moratorium on foreign debt\par payments by Russian banks and companies\par    -- Russian President Boris Yeltsin has rejected top\par financial officials' offers to leave their posts, the\par Interfax news agency reported, citing an unnamed government\par source.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  08-18-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1807EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4223 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8i020bg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
