\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr sheds gains, market mulls Russia policy change. \b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1998\par 13:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 17 (Reuters) - The dollar was near its session lows against the mark at Europe's midsession, giving up the ground it gained earlier on Russia's decision to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. While prospects of turmoil in the Russian markets were likely to bolster the dollar for now, its direction would be determined by how Moscow followed up what was, in effect, a rouble devaluation, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 1.7982/87 marks at 1040 GMT, off the five-week high it reached earlier and almost unchanged from levels seen in late European trade on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We've had the knee-jerk reaction to the Russian news and now there's damage limitation going on with people saying that while Germany's more exposed than other countries to Russia, the exposure's not that large," said Mark Cliffe, international economist at HSBC. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark moved about 1.5 pfennigs, a range that some traders and analysts said was relatively small considering the weight of the news out of Moscow. \par \par \uc2  The Russian government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was impractical to keep the daily rouble corridor and said they would let the markets determine the rate. \par \par \uc2  It also declared a 90-day moratorium on repaying foreign credits. \par \par \uc2  Noting that the effective devaluation had been well anticipated, analysts said market moves would depend on how Moscow dealt with other problems ravaging the economy. \par \par \uc2  "One key issue is the fiscal situation and how the government is progressing with tax collection, and another is the Russian banking system," said Dick Howard, director of economic research at Julius Baer Investments. "But I think the dollar will continue to have a fairly firm tone." \par \par \uc2  Markets would now keep a close eye on the attitude of Russia's lower house of parliament, the Duma, when it meets in an emergency session on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We'll have to see if they have any appetite to address the fiscal problems," said Cliffe at HSBC. \par \par \uc2  Before that, traders will be looking out for U.S. President Bill Clinton's testimony to the grand jury, due at 1700 GMT on Monday, regarding his relationship with former White House intern Monica Lewinsky. \par \par \uc2  However, the Lewinsky issue was not expected to have much impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "There may be a knee-jerk reaction to it, but it's a story that has been going on for seven months or so," Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  The yen was on the back burner, with traders saying Russia was diverting the market's attention from the woes of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's depressed economy. \par \par \uc2  While the fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was preventing dollar bulls from taking dollar/yen toward last week's eight-year high, analysts said it would not be long before the yen's downtrend resumed. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was quoted at 146.12/22 yen, down about one percent from the multi-year high of 147.63. \par \par \uc2  The British pound slumped to its weakest in seven months against the dollar due to the latter's safe-haven strength on the back of developments in Russia. Sterling/mark was firm due to the German \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness across the board, but was weighed down by sterling/dollar's weakness. \par \par \uc2  The pound was at $1.6133/43, off its low of $1.6085 but down about 0.5 percent from late Friday. Sterling/mark was at 2.9007/17 after rising to 2.9237 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8h00xp8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
