\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Stocks Open Sharply Lower Amid Arbitrage Trading \b0\par\par\uc2 372 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 02:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Tokyo stock market is opening lower Friday amid arbitrage trading related to the settlement of August Nikkei options contracts and a weakening yen. \par \par \uc2  At 0046 GMT (8:46 p.m. EDT Thursday), the Nikkei average of 225 selected issues was at 15,152.80, down 229.22 points. \par \par \uc2  August Nikkei options contracts were settled at 15,231.72 Friday, down 150.30 points from the previous close of the cash Nikkei average, market sources said. \par \par \uc2  Before the opening, investors were net sellers of Japanese stocks at 11 foreign brokerages market sources said, quoted by Kyodo news agency. There were 18.5 million shares of sell orders against 17.5 million of buys. \par \par \uc2  Traders say they will be keeping a close eye on movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and stock trading in Hong Kong. A fall in Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index to its lowest level in five years Thursday was a negative factor for the Tokyo stock market Thursday, which nevertheless managed to end an eight-day losing streak as the Nikkei average blipped up 3.05 points. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the dollar is trading around Y145.75 in Tokyo early Friday. Earlier in the morning, it was below Y145.00. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they are concerned the yen will continue to weaken further as the main factor that seems to be supporting it now is talk of yen-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese government. \par \par \uc2  Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami said that the yen's exchange rate against the dollar is currently too weak given Japan's huge current account surplus, which rose 46.2% from the year-earlier month to Y1.444 trillion in June. But traders say that Japan's weak economy is another fundamental relevant to the yen's value. \par \par \uc2  Sakura Bank shares are Y5 higher at Y285. Friday, The Mainichi Shimbun reported Friday morning that the bank would reduce its overseas lending significantly and reduce the number of overseas offices to about 50 from the current 94 by 2001. \par \par \uc2  -By James Paradise, 813-5255-2947, jparadise@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 14-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0059GMT \par \par \uc2  4159 5011 55117 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8e06wig\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
