\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Russia, BoJ play tug-of-dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 18:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 13 (Reuters) - A tug-of-war between Russian financial turmoil and expectations of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled the dollar in opposite directions against the mark and yen on Thursday, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Speculation of a rouble devaluation drove dollar/mark to a one-month high in early trade, while senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official Eisuke Sakakibara started a dollar/yen fall from above 147 to 145 in morning Europe. \par \par \uc2  "Threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been the main factor behind dollar/yen losses today," said David Brickman, international economist at PaineWebber. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/mark came off in reaction to a letter in the Financial Times about rouble revaluation," Brickman said. "The fear of that happening receded during the day." \par \par \uc2  By 1405 GMT dollar/mark was at 1.7837/47 from an early high at 1.8003 and versus 1.7750/60 in late European trade on Wednesday. By afternoon Europe dollar/yen had seen 144.60, it was last at 145.05/10 compared to 146.01/11 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets nervous about yen-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  started profit-taking on long dollar/yen positions after Sakakibara said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was looking for chances to conduct yen-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Brickman at PaineWebber said the mark drop in early trade was a direct reaction to a letter in the Financial Times newspaper from international financier George Soros. \par \par \uc2  Soros called for a devaluation of the rouble and suggested the introduction of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegged to the dollar or euro. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said German export and banking exposure left the mark as the most vulnerable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Russian problems. \par \par \uc2  Russian share prices slid sharply again on Thursday, while trading was suspended for a short while in the morning. One-month GKO t-bill yields rose over 200 percent at one stage from between 28-55 percent on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc also rose briefly to eight-week highs versus the mark as safe-haven flows into Swiss assets escalated while investor confidence in Russia ebbed. \par \par \uc2  By 1545 GMT the mark was at 83.33/36 Swiss centimes from a Thursday low at 83.13 centimes and versus 83.55/58 centimes late Wednesday. Dollar/Swiss franc was at 1.4878/88 against 1.4832/42 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Soros letter followed the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statements on Wednesday that interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade was grinding to a halt due to lack of liquidity and to a crisis of confidence between banks. \par \par \uc2  Mark levels ticked up after the German finance ministry said the G7 was as usual in contact about Russia. \par \par \uc2  A German source said he does not expect a devaluation of the rouble and that no special G7 meeting with Russia was expected. \par \par \uc2  In the mark's favour was a comment by Moody's Investor Services. A credit analyst said the economic situation in Russia was not enough to cause a change in German Bank credit ratings. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the G7 will be prepared to help Russia out with its difficulties, unless a rouble devaluation is agreed," Lewis at Monument Derivatives said. \par \par \uc2  ((Pratima Desai, London newsroom +44 171 542 2737)). \par \par \uc2