\par\par \b \uc2  'Mr Yen' lone voice of optimism on Japan economy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By George Nishiyama \par \uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 12:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, sounded an upbeat note on the economy on Thursday, saying he expected it to turn around soon, but his was a lone voice amid signs of continued gloom. \par \par \uc2  "I think a turning point for Japan's economy will come in August-September," said Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, the Bank of Japan (BOJ) in a monthly report on Thursday painted a much darker picture. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's economic conditions continue to deteriorate," the BOJ report said, adding that job and income conditions had worsened further and final demand remained weak. \par \par \uc2  "Even if, hypothetically, the economy improved from autumn, the base is so low that large growth is impossible" for the year through next March, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said. \par \par \uc2  Hayami told a news conference he did not think the economy would reach the government's official target of 1.9 percent growth for the fiscal year. He said he looked forward to how Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya, who has called that target impossible, would revise the official forecast. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said production, incomes and spending remained in a negative cycle, capital spending continued to fall sharply, personal consumption was not improving and large production cutbacks were continuing. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ also did not pin high hopes on the government's economic stimulus steps, either those already implemented or those promised by new Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi. \par \par \uc2  Although the BOJ said a 16 trillion yen ($109 billion) package of economic steps announced by the government in late April would halt a further worsening of the economy, it said the package would fall short of bringing about a quick transition to a "self-sustained" recovery. \par \par \uc2  Regarding tax cuts and additional public works spending promised by Obuchi, the BOJ said it would "carefully monitor" their effects on corporate and household sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Economists said expectations of an economic upturn in the next two months were wishful thinking. \par \par \uc2  Hisashi Yamada, an economist at Japan Research Institute, said the government economic package would not be able to turn around the worsening of capital spending and job conditions. \par \par \uc2  "There is a risk that capital spending and jobs - the two engines of the economy - may worsen further.... Public works may increase in the autumn, but (the package) will not cancel out (the negative effects of the worsening)," Yamada said. \par \par \uc2  Kazuto Uchida, chief strategist at Tokyo-Mitsubishi Securities, said the BOJ may be forced to revise downward its economic assessment in the future due to weak capital spending and consumer sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Many economists discounted Sakakibara's remarks as an attempt to shore up the yen, currently at eight-year lows, rather than a carefully thought out forecast on the economy. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said the August-September period will be a turning point for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was looking for chances to conduct yen supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "He had to make those comments because of his position," said an economist who declined to be named. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities, faced with a plummeting yen, have repeatedly said a weak yen was detrimental not only to Japan's economy but also to the global economy, as it leads to current account imbalances and trade friction. \par \par \uc2  BOJ chief Hayami said: "If possible, a strong yen would be favourable," adding that it would be in Japan's interests. \par \par \uc2  But he offered no quick fix, saying the yen was weak largely because market participants thought Japan was not progressing well with economic recovery and financial-system rehabilitation. \par \par \uc2  "If improvement can be made on these points, I expect the yen would get a bit stronger," Hayami said. \par \par \uc2  Although declining specific comment on the dollar's push above 147.00 yen on Thursday - near the eight-year high it set on Tuesday - he said it could be argued that the yen was "over-depreciated", given Japan's large current account surplus. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry reported on Thursday that the current account surplus rose 46.2 percent in June from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara's morning comments boosted the yen, but it had slipped back by late in the day. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped briefly as high as 145.00 to the dollar in skittish London trading around 6 p.m. (0900 GMT) on rumours that the BOJ was checking dealers' dollar/yen rates. ($1=146 yen) ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8d031f3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
