\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends U.S. lower,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears linger. \b0\par\par\uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 22:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The dollar fell against the yen Thursday as investors remained leery of possible Japanese official yen buying after Japanese officials overnight threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark edged lower on continuing profit-taking that began after a spike higher to 1.80 overnight, though a late session comment on Russia from renowned international financier George Soros gave the greenback a brief boost, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Soros seems to be what did it. His comments here and overnight in the Financial Times were driving the market over the course of the day," said a U.S. investment bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Soros issued a statement saying he hoped major global economic powers would come to Russia's aid as it struggled to overcome a severe cash shortage. \par \par \uc2  He called his letter in Thursday's Financial Times a "wake-up call" for the Group of Seven nations. That letter urged Russia to devalue its battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rouble, and set up a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. \par \par \uc2  The White House confirmed that finance officials from the G7 on Thursday held a telephone conference call to discuss Russia and that top U.S. financial officials had been in contact with Russian leaders. \par \par \uc2  Ratings agencies Moody's Investors Service and Standard and Poor's Corp. each downgraded Russia on Thursday, though Russia has carried a low-junk status for some time and investors paid little heed to the actions. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended lower at 1.7845/50 marks from 1.7875/78 at the open. Dollar/yen dipped to 144.95/00 from 145.55/59 at the open and 146.15/25 at Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  Concern about yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  heightened overnight after Eisuke Sakakibara, a senior Japanese Finance Ministry official known for his ability to move \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was looking for chances to conduct yen-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers also said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  checked market rates in dollar/yen above 147 overnight, causing investors to dump dollars for fear the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might ultimately sell greenbacks for yen. \par \par \uc2  "People now want to take profits at these levels. They must be taking the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threat seriously-the dollar doesn't normally go down two big figures when the BoJ checks rates," said Hans Boman, chief spot dealer at Swedbank in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit an eight-year high at 147.63 yen on Tuesday but has failed to move higher since than as investors have booked profits from a sharp dollar rally in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's long-term upward trend seemed intact, with dealers stressing no amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could stop the flood of capital to the United States and out of battered Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  "It's foolish to think that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can have anything but a short-term impact. Given the economic fundamentals, it's not going to turn things around," said one dealer. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the Canadian dollar, reportedly buying at about C$1.52 to the dollar. But the greenback recovered and continued to move back toward record highs against its Canadian counterpart. \par \par \uc2  "Since last week, the Bank of Canada has used up about $2 billion of reserves and the market has taken that as an opportunity to go out and buy (U.S.) dollars," said Frank Conte, chief dealer at Royal Bank of Canada in New York." \par \par \uc2  "There is still a shortage of U.S. dollars there, and the market is saying 'thanks very much.'" \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to Canadian $1.5198/03 from C$1.5155/60 at the open and edged higher to 1.4890/00 Swiss francs from 1.4888/98. Sterling dipped to $1.6240/45 from $1.6242/52. The Australian dollar rose to $0.5964/69 from $0.5953/58. \par \par \uc2  ((-Chris Stetkiewicz, 212-859-1664)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8d03137\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
