\par\par \b \uc2  Firmer dollar fails to build on gains in Europe. \b0\par\par\uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 10:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The dollar was firmer in European morning trade on Thursday, boosted by jitters about Russia's financial problems and sharp losses in Hong Kong stocks, but profit-taking knocked the U.S. unit off highs. \par \par \uc2  Speculation about the fate of the Russian rouble was heightened by a suggestion from international financier George Soros that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  be devalued and pegged to the dollar or euro. \par \par \uc2  But a blip above 1.80 marks for the first time in a month failed to generate follow-through buying in a generally thin market, while fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  knocked the dollar off its perch above 147 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The escalating concern about Russia has given the dollar a boost and weakened the mark on the crossess," said Rob Miniken, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London. "But market conditions are very light, so the extent of the move might have been exaggerated." \par \par \uc2  The dollar eased against the mark after a senior Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Soros's suggestion would not solve the country's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  At 0730 GMT the dollar was at 1.7895/7900 marks and 146.49/59 yen, off highs at 1.8003 and 147.18 but up from 1.7750/60 and 146.01/11 in late European trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The mark, vunerable to Russian jitters due to the exposure of German banks, weakened to an eight-week low against the safe-haven Swiss franc at 83.18 centimes. Sterling hit a two-week high at 2.9288 marks on general mark weakness. \par \par \uc2  The market became more nervous after Russian stocks extended their sharp falls on Wednesday. Jitters intensified after Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would put a limit on the amount of dollars local banks can purchase in the interbank market. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the mark sell-off in response to Soros' proposal, published in a letter to the Financial Times on Thursday, was likely to be short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "There is no huge concern that Russia's problems are going to push the mark over the edge," said Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in London. \par \par \uc2  Yet, the dollar eased off its 1.7877 mark low after Russian shares opened sharply lower on Thursday. Russia's markets RTS share trade was then suspended at 0740 GMT, according to Naufor Brokers Association. The Russian securities markets watchdog had recommended suspending share trade. Some key stocks shed 16 and 13 percent respectively at the open. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen the dollar eased back into the mid-146 yen area after caution set in when senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official Eisuke Sakakibara said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was looking for chances to conduct yen-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A sharp fall in Hong Kong's Hang Seng share index by 2.9 percent weighed on the yen even though it gained some support from the Nikkei's resilience as Tokyo's benchmark index closed little changed, ending an eight-session losing streak. \par \par \uc2  But fears that the yen's prolonged weakness could prompt central banks in other countries to reduce yen in their external reserves offset some of that support, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trimming the yen portion of its reserves to ensure maximum returns, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  department chief Chou Ah-ting said on Thursday. He declined to specify the amount of the reduction. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's action followed a similar report about mainland China earlier this week. The Financial Times said on Tuesday China has sharply reduced its yen-denominated reserve holdings to protect the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  The market was unmoved by news that Japan's current account surplusrose 46.2 percent in June from a year ago to 1.44 trillion yen, with the trade surplus rising 25.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said "one could say the yen was over-depreciated" given Japan's large current account surplus and that progress on financial system reform should boost the yen. U.S. retail sales data will be released at 1230 GMT, along with weekly jobless claims. Retail sales are expected to have dropped 0.8 percent in July after a 0.1 percent monthly rise in June. The expected drop is partly due to the General Motors strike, and retail sales are seen up 0.4 percent excluding auto sales. ((Astrid Zweynert London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8d030s3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
