\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis/Glance:Dlr Resumes Rise; \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Eyes  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 10:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- The dollar tested Y147 in Asian trade as worries of joint U.S.-Japan action to support the yen diminished and worries of Russia's financial crisis rose. At 0820 GMT, the dollar was at Y146.44, up from Y146.29 late Wednesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minister Sakakibara said August and September are "turning point" months for the economy, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is waiting for an opportunity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during those months. \par \par \uc2  -- Citing the weakening of the yen and low returns on yen-denominated assets, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut yen holdings to 6.5% from 15% of total reserves, according to a local newspaper. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong's Hang Seng index closed down 2.9% at 6660.42 on worries over a weakening yen and a sell-off of HSBC Holdings. \par \par \uc2  -- Malaysian stocks were down 4.4% late on a weakening ringgit, widening corporate woes and domestic political worries. Singapore stocks followed Malaysia, down 3% late. \par \par \uc2  -- Japan's seasonally unadjusted current account surplus for June rose 46% from June 1997 to Y1.444 trillion. \par \par \uc2  -- Thousands of South Korean automobile, ship and machinery workers say they'll begin a general strike if the government sends riot police into Hyundai Motor plants, where workers have been striking for three weeks to protest layoffs. \par \par \uc2  -- The Bank of Japan provided a bleaker economic outlook in its latest monthly report, noting in particular worsening employment conditions. \par \par \uc2  -- With China's exports continuing to slide, the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  renewed pledges to increase loans to exporters, state media said. \par \par \uc2  -- The number of Australians regarded as long-term unemployed rose 7.4% in July from June. \par \par \uc2  -- Malaysia's ringgit is on track to match or surpass historic lows as authorities show their willingness to sacrifice the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by pumping money into the sinking economy, economists and dealers said, predicting MYR4.50 or lower by year's end. The ringgit, trading at MYR4.2200, is down 40% against the dollar since July 1997. \par \par \uc2  -- A growing logjam of troubled Malaysian companies is raising concerns about the ability of the country's legal system to handle a wave of financial breakdowns. Property outfit Kemayan Corp. became the latest to receive court protection from creditors. \par \par \uc2  -- Moody's put Malaysian national electricity utility Tenaga Nasional's senior debt on review for possible cut after the company said it might delay payments to independent power producers.S&P said it doesn't believe Tenaga is facing a short-term liquidity crisis. \par \par \uc2  -- Some NT$80 billion of Taiwan government-owned shares from previous budgets can be sold to help fund the government's domestic stimulus package, said a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 13-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0853GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 55126 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8d06v4e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
