\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar soft amid reports Japan wants higher yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 13:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 12 (Reuters) - The softer dollar took a turn for the worse at the European midsession on Wednesday amid reports that Japanese authorities see a much lower dollar/yen rate as appropriate, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell almost one yen after various news agencies reported that an unnamed Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official said an exchange rate of about 130 to 139 yen was acceptable. \par \par \uc2  Kyodo news agency reported the official said a dollar/yen rate of about 130 yen to the dollar was appropriate. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, the yen was also buoyed by reports that U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin and Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa will meet in San Francisco on September 4 and 5. \par \par \uc2  "These developments are adding to the market's more cautious mood," said Claudio Piron, treasury economist at Standard Chartered in London. "Even though one could see Rubin/Miyazawa meeting merely as a reactive type of policy, it has to be seen in the context of increased cooperation." \par \par \uc2  At 1030 GMT the dollar was at 145.09/19 yen, below its Asian trough of 145.45 and down from 147.25/30 in late Europe Tuesday. Dollar/mark was at 1.7728/38, just above overnight lows and versus 1.7810/15 late Tuesday. Mark/yen was at 82.09/12, off Tuesday's 5-1/2 year high at 82.80 and from 82.68/71. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had weakened sharply overnight, dragged down by a corrective bounce in the yen and amid caution about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its drop to fresh eight-year lows against the dollar at 147.63 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said even though no joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was expected at this stage, speculation was likely to intensify ahead of the Rubin/Miyazwa meeting. \par \par \uc2  The news of the meeting came after the White House said yesterday that President Bill Clinton and U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin spoke about latest gyrations in financial markets during a telephone call on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Clinton and Japanese Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi are expected to meet in the U.S. from September 21. \par \par \uc2  Concern that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew after the yen's fall stoked jitters in Asian and global financial markets. Asian financial markets avoided further losses on Wednesday thanks to the firmer yen even though traders said the tone was still defensive. \par \par \uc2  Speculation about a Chinese yuan devaluation is likely to keep simmering, analysts said. China's ambassador to India said on Wednesday that his country may be forced to rethink its "no devaluation" stand if the yen falls further. \par \par \uc2  The yen's weakness prompted verbal support from several senior Japanese officials and Prime Minister Obuchi. \par \par \uc2  "The concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stay in place for the time being and cap the dollar's upside against the yen," said Ian Gunner, treasury economist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had already started to ease before Jiji news agency quoted Eisuke Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), as saying Japan will adopt an aggressive stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when necessary was unchanged and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be a trigger for a global recession. The yen's fall was not to blame for global stock price falls, he said. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi and Haruhiko Kuroda, another senior MOF official, expressed concern about the weak yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers will be keeping a close eye on U.S. stocks after the yen's slide contributed to a 1.3 percent drop in the Dow Jones. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said any further weakness in U.S. stocks and jittery Russian markets were likely to pull dollar/mark in opposite directions, making it even less likely that the pair would break out of its 1.7550-1.7950 range. \par \par \uc2  Sterling took the Bank of England's quarterly inflation report and British wage data in its stride but got hit by a comment from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deputy governor Mervin King. He said he expected the pound to fall, particularly against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark was at 2.8892/8902, compared with 2.9055/65 in late Europe on Tuesday and at $1.6286/96 from $1.6310/20. \par \par \uc2  ((Astrid Zweynert London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8c02yru\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
