\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Yen rise gives Asia stocks some respite. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mark Snelling \par \uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 12:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 12 (Reuters) - The yen rallied and gave Asia's battered share markets a chance to lick their wounds on Wednesday, but worries over global market falls and the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outlook took their toll on some shares. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong gained 1.2 percent and Tokyo closed just a little lower thanks to the dollar falling by two yen during the morning to a low of 145.45. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was hovering below 146 yen in early European trade. The yen had slid to eight-year lows on Tuesday, sparking a regional - then global - share sell-off. \par \par \uc2  Rhetoric from Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi, among other officials, helped buoy the yen, as did growing caution over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although Tokyo's Nikkei index closed just 0.2 percent lower at 15,379.0, dealers said investors had too many doubts that Obuchi's new government would get Japan's economy going again, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Markets are not giving Obuchi the benefit of the doubt because they've been burned to many times in the past," said Ron Bevacqua, an economist at Merrill Lynch Japan. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong gained as investors saw bargains in a market that had fallen to a five-year low on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The territory's Hang Seng Index added 79.5 points to finish at 6,859.5, but brokers expected the market to stay fairly volatile in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian stocks similarly regained some ground from Tuesday's 10-year low and ended 2.2 percent higher at 342.2, although some traders said the market remained fundamentally bearish. \par \par \uc2  "The Composite Index may be up but the broad market is still bad," said a dealer with a bank-backed brokerage. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs, Eisuke Sakakibara, told reporters on Tuesday there was no change in the Japanese government's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when deemed necessary. \par \par \uc2  Those remarks helped propel the yen higher, partly balancing the negative impact of big overnight falls on U.S. and European markets which were themselves prompted by worries over Asia's shaky economies. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said other regional markets were still anxious about the frail Japanese and Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and had been hurt by the Dow's fall of more than 100 points. \par \par \uc2  Manila stocks followed up Tuesday's near four percent loss with a 4.8 percent dive, as lack of confidence in the country's new government compounded anxiety over the regional turmoil. Some analysts were sceptical that the yen's strength would last, given the weakness of the Japanese economy and perceived reluctance by the U.S. to help Tokyo in any dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Seoul shares refused to get excited about the yen's gains. The main index closed down nearly two percent at 305.35, with traders citing lingering uncertainty over the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  And despite slightly higher prices in Hong Kong, hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B shares over the border in Shenzhen took a beating as poor corporate results hit sentiment, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The B-share index ended down just over five percent at a record low close of 50.08. Shanghai's equivalent bourse also posted an all-time low. \par \par \uc2  Singapore lost 1.2 percent, closing at 995.8, while the New Zealand market fell 2.05 percent to 1,952.4. \par \par \uc2  Manila's slump was led by a massive nine percent fall in the market's property index, a response to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp tightening of interest rates this week. \par \par \uc2  The main index fell to 1,307.3 points, but off the day's low of 1,263.0 points. \par \par \uc2  "Earlier in the session, people panicked...There was programmed selling on the foreign side because of the fall of the U.S. market," said a senior trader of a foreign brokerage. \par \par \uc2  Stocks in Indonesia gained slightly on the back of healthier blue-chip shares. Thai markets were closed for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan stocks took a slight knock from Wall Street, ending down 0.44 percent, while nervy Australian stocks gave up a similar amount to close at 2,551.0. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8c02yqy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
