\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr soft in Europe on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fear,yen bounce. \b0\par\par\uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 12 (Reuters) - The dollar kicked off weaker in Europe on Wednesday and was expected to struggle on the upside after a corrective bounce in the yen and amid caution about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns grew after the yen's fall to fresh eight-year lows against the dollar on Tuesday stoked jitters in Asian and global financial markets. \par \par \uc2  Rhetoric in support of the yen from several senior Japanese officials and Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi also buoyed the yen. It rose over two yen from Tuesday's low of 147.63 to the dollar to a high of 145.45 and was hovering near 146 in morning Europe. \par \par \uc2  "The concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stay in place for the time being and cap the dollar's upside against the yen," said Ian Gunner, treasury economist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had already started to ease before Jiji news agency quoted Eisuke Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), as saying Japan will adopt an aggressive stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when necessary was unchanged and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be a trigger for a global recession. The yen's fall was not to blame for global stock price falls, he said. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Obuchi and Haruhiko Kuroda, another senior MOF official, expressed concern about the weak yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar was quite top-heavy after the rise to new highs, so the comments added pressure on the downside," said Gunner. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said profit-taking on dollar/yen triggered a round of stop-loss selling from 146.90 in Asia. The sudden fall sparked unconfirmed rumours of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 0800 GMT, the dollar was at 145.90/146.00 yen and 1.7782/92 marks, down from 147.25/30 and 1.7810/15 in late Europe on Tuesday. Mark/yen was. \par \par \uc2  Dealers will be keeping a close eye on stock markets, which adopted a calmer tone after the yen's slide on Tuesday added to a 1.4 percent tumble in the Nikkei index and also contributed to a 1.3 percent drop in the Dow Jones industrial average. \par \par \uc2  "The curency markets will be looking for their cue from stock markets for short-term direction," said Sally Wilkinson, senior economist at Daiwa Europe. \par \par \uc2  Asian financial markets avoided further losses thanks to the firmer yen even though traders said the tone was still defensive. Wilkinson said the dollar's upside was likely to be limited due \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns, but possibly not for long. \par \par \uc2  "The impact is likely to be short-lived until we see verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the U.S. or actual BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Wilkinson. \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials have been quiet lately about the weakness of the yen. The White House said President Bill Clinton and U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin spoke on the telephone on Tuesday. Officials refused to specify what Clinton and Rubin discussed but said wobbly financial markets were a factor. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said any further weakness in U.S. stocks and jittery Russian markets were likely to pull dollar/mark in opposite directions within its recent 1.7750-1.7900 range. \par \par \uc2  Sterling's performance after British jobs data, due at 0830 GMT, and the Bank of England's quarterly inflation report at 0930 GMT, will be closely eyed. \par \par \uc2  "Any suggestion of a softening stance on inflation in the BOE's report will be negative for sterling," said Gunner at ABN AMRO. \par \par \uc2  In its previous report, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been at pains to point to inflation risks but now it had to consider a lot of data suggesting a hard landing for the British economy, Gunner added. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark was little changed at 2.9053/63 and firmer against a generally softer dolllar at $1.6345/55. \par \par \uc2  ((Astrid Zweynert London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8c02yiy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
