\par\par \b \uc2  Japan, US unlikely to boost yen, yuan - analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 16:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Reuters) - Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buoy the yen and save China's yuan is unlikely, and even if it occurs it may not relieve pressure on China to devalue, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  With the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  scraping eight-year lows, financial market participants are concerned China might feel stronger pressure to devalue the yuan, they said. \par \par \uc2  Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States in mid-June temporarily eased pressure on China, but even if they do so again, such action is not seen as an instant remedy to soothe the situation in China, they said. \par \par \uc2  "It's not unthinkable, but I doubt that the United States and Japan will act jointly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ease pressure on the yuan," a senior Japanese bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may help boost the yen, but to what extent a higher yen can be effective for the yuan is not known. China has its own problems that are pressuring its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. \par \par \uc2  China has repeatedly said it is determined not to devalue the yuan, but growing concerns have depressed Asian financial markets since last week. \par \par \uc2  Liu Mingkang, deputy governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of China, said Tuesday: "The renminbi (yuan) does not need to be devalued and will not be devalued." \par \par \uc2  Daisuke Hiratsuka, deputy head of the econometric analysis and forecasting division at the Institute of Developing Economies said a devaluation of the yuan was highly unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "China is pretty much aware what the consequences will be after devaluation, following the results of devaluation in Southeast Asia last year," Hiratsuka said. "I doubt that China will rush to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon." \par \par \uc2  He added: "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the yuan is also unlikely. There is no clear evidence that a stronger yen would definitely improve the situation in China. Another question is whether the U.S. would allow the dollar to depreciate sharply." \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve and the Bank of Japan stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market just before Chinese President Jiang Zemin's visit to the United States in June. But with the U.S. stock market recently unstable, and Japan unclear on how it will solve its banks' bad loan problems, there is no clear reason for the two countries to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again, they said. \par \par \uc2  With the yuan facing speculative attack, China has defended the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but may have little choice but to devalue to stimulate its economy, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  China's economic growth slowed to an official 7 percent rate year-on-year in the first half of 1998, its lowest rate since 1991. Analysts said growth has to pick up dramatically in the second half of the year in order to achieve the country's annual growth target of 8 percent. \par \par \uc2  They said China's slowing growth could raise unemployment at state-owned firms and eventually push it into recession in the next year. \par \par \uc2  Beijing may also want Japan to improve its economy through stronger exports, so China can in turn bolster its exports to a revived Japan market, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Last week, China's parliament chief Li Peng urged Japan's Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi and his new cabinet to ease pressure on China by invigorating the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, China's official Economic Information Daily urged the United States and Japan to support the yen and help Asia avert a new round of economic crises. \par \par \uc2  "The United States and Japan should adopt resolute actions to stop the downward trend of the Japanese yen to help some Asian countries navigate out of troubled water," the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's movement is being closely monitored by the market to determine the fate of the yuan, but it will be up to Beijing to make the final decision, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "I think the yen's fluctuation will not have an influence on the yuan. If China wants to devalue, I think they wouldn't care where the yen stands. The country will simply do it (devalue the yuan) based on its own decision," said Mitsuru Saito, deputy general manager at Sanwa Bank's treasury section. \par \par \uc2  Hirofumi Ushikoshi, senior economist at NLI Research Institute, said: "I think devaluation is the best alternative for China to stimulate its economy, and there is growing demand to do so within the country. I feel there is a great chance of seeing a devaluation." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8c02yam\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
