\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Yen's rise cushions Asia from Dow fall-out. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stuart Grudgings \par \uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 10:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 12 (Reuters) - A jump in the yen's value gave Asia's battered markets a chance to lick their wounds on Wednesday, but worries over global market falls and the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outlook took their toll on some shares. \par \par \uc2  The yen, whose slide to eight-year lows to the dollar on Tuesday contributed to a regional, then global, share sell-off, rebounded by around two yen in the morning amid growing caution over a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's Nikkei index closed only slightly down, and the region's other linchpin exchange, Hong Kong, ended the first session with small gains. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian stocks had clawed back some of Tuesday's heavy losses with a near three percent rise by midday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yen's rapid rise to as high as 145.40 to the dollar had helped balance the negative impact of big overnight falls on U.S. and European markets, themselves prompted by worries over Asia's shaky economies. \par \par \uc2  But they said other regional markets were still anxious about the frail Japanese and Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and had been hurt by the Dow's more than 100-point fall. \par \par \uc2  Manila stocks followed up Tuesday's near four percent loss with a 4.8 percent dive. Singapore, Korean and Chinese stocks were also on the ropes. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment is negative because of the decline in the U.S. market overnight, volatility in the peso and rise in domestic interest rates," said Fitzgerald Aclan, assistant vice-president at Orion Squire Capital Inc, explaining Manila's fall. \par \par \uc2  Asian shares and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled across the board on Tuesday, spooked by fears that a weakening Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might prompt China to devalue its yuan and send regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  crashing. \par \par \uc2  The yen's rise partly soothed such nerves, but some analysts were sceptical whether it would last, given the weakness of the Japanese economy and perceived reluctance by the U.S. to help Tokyo in any dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Seoul shares refused to get excited about the yen's gains. The main index was down nearly two percent at 305.35 in the afternoon, with traders citing lingering uncertainty over the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara told reporters on Tuesday there was no change in the Japanese government's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when deemed necessary. \par \par \uc2  The yen's abrupt rise after those remarks gave Tokyo and Hong Kong shares a much-needed breather. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are not keen to buy after prices rose higher earlier this morning, but there is also no excuse to sell heavily amid a strengthened yen," said Terry Cheung, sales director at Core Pacific-Yamaichi in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei index ended at 15,378.97, down just 0.18 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The Dow's close off its low and the yen's recovery after Sakakibara comments kept the Nikkei average from diving sharply," said Masatoshi Sato, manager at Kankaku Securities. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks pared gains to trade slightly higher in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index edged up 9.35 points, or 0.14 percent, to finish the morning at 6,789.30 after swinging between 6,708.18 and 6,885.79 points. \par \par \uc2  Over the territory's border, hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B shares in Shenzhen took a beating as poor corporate results hit sentiment, traders said. Its main index ended the morning down four percent, with Shanghai's equivalent bourse also losing over three percent. \par \par \uc2  Manila's slump was led by a massive nine percent fall in the market's property index following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp tightening of interest rates this week. \par \par \uc2  The main index fell 4.8 percent to 1,307.30 points, but off the day's low of 1,263.03 points. \par \par \uc2  "Earlier in the session, people panicked...There was programmed selling on the foreign side because of the fall of the U.S. market," said a senior trader of a foreign brokerage. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian markets took heart from a slightly firmer ringgit after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent weakness. But dealers remained doubtful whether the share index's rise was anything more than a technical rebound from Tuesday's plunge. \par \par \uc2  The composite index was up 9.13 points, or 2.73 percent, at 343.83 at midday as the ringgit rose to 4.2150 per dollar from 4.2450 earlier. \par \par \uc2  Other Southeast Asian markets were mixed, Singapore shares falling by around 1.50 percent and stocks in Indonesia gaining slightly on the back of healthier blue-chip shares. Thai markets were closed for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan stocks took a slight knock from Wall Street, ending down 0.44 percent, while nervy Australian stocks gave up a similar amount. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8c02y3k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
