\par\par \b \uc2  Taiwan stocks end down on Wall St angst, yen woes. \b0\par\par\uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 07:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TAIPEI, Aug 12 (Reuters) - Overnight stock falls on Wall Street and around the world rattled Taiwan's share market on Wednesday, but the Japanese yen's modest recovery from Tuesday's eight-year low helped to limit the damage, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  * Close: down 32.47 points/0.44 percent at 7,351.51 \par \par \uc2  * Turnover: muted at T$67.2 billion vs T$63.37 billion \par \par \uc2  * Index fell sharply from the opening, dropping as much as 1.46 percent in response to overnight stock downturns worldwide - especially the U.S. Dow Jones index's 1.31 percent fall and a 2.53 percent tumble on the technologies-laden Nasdaq index. \par \par \uc2  * Early sentiment also hurt by the yen's fall on Tuesday to fresh eight-year lows, which hammered Taiwan's dollar to an eight-week closing low of T$34.81 and triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  * Bargain-hunting gradually emerged as both the yen and the Taiwan dollar rebounded in Wednesday morning trade. \par \par \uc2  * Late buying on the trend-setting electronics enabled the index to close at its intraday high, though still below its Tuesday close. \par \par \uc2  As of Wednesday's close, the index has fallen 10 percent in calendar 1998, but remains 3.3 percent above its 1998 closing low of 7,117.11 marked on June 12. The index has lost 21 percent from its 1998 closing high of 9,277.09 on March 2. \par \par \uc2  "Wall Street's tumbles and the yen jitters further dampened the already sluggish Taiwan index," said HSBC Securities assistant vice-president Alex Chen. \par \par \uc2  "Athough the yen's morning rebound helped limit the Taiwan index's fall, investors still felt uncertain and pessimistic about the outlook for the yen and for Wall Street," Chen said. \par \par \uc2  Chen said unless another round of U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boosts the yen strongly, the Taiwan index likely will continue on its recent downtrend. \par \par \uc2  Beyond Asset Management president Michael On took a contrary view, saying investors need not be so pessimistic. \par \par \uc2  "Further downside should be limited because Wall Street is only in a downward correction and is not expected to tumble further," On said. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's downside is also limited because, though Japan's economy has not seen improvement, neither has it worsened," On said. \par \par \uc2  Brokers noted strong support around 7,000, believing the government will step in with stimulus measures to boost the market if the index falls close to the level. \par \par \uc2  For Thursday, a 7,370-7,420 range was seen. \par \par \uc2  Electronics, which fell as much as 2.41 percent, pared their losses sharply to end down only 0.29 percent. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said investors bought the closely watched sector in late trade on expectations it would see rising orders in its upcoming hot-sale season between August and November. \par \par \uc2  Computer giant Acer  rose T$0.9 to T$38.1. \par \par \uc2  The heavily weighted financials fell 1.88 percent, but foods rose 2.42 percent. Car and cement shares also managed gains. \par \par \uc2  Food share Taiwan Pineapple , an asset-backed stock that has been the target of speculation, rose the daily seven percent to T$114. ((Taipei newsroom, +886 2 2508-0815 fax +886 2 2508-0204, taipei.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8c02xc4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
