\par\par \b \uc2  Weak yen won't promp Fed dlr sales now - US dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 1998\par 23:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 11 (Reuters) - Fresh fears about Asia's financial crisis sparked talk of if and when central banks may try to stabilise the yen, but U.S. dealers doubted Washington would be willing to extend Tokyo a helping hand now. \par \par \uc2  "The United States is not going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not for a while," predicted John McCarthy, senior vice president at ING Barings Capital Market, arguing the time is not right. \par \par \uc2  While the dollar has gained over four percent against the yen since Washington and Tokyo jointly sold dollars for yen in June, speculation about more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  resurfaced only now as the dollar hit its highest level in eight years at 146.75. \par \par \uc2  In early European trading, the yen crumbled further to reach a low of 147.63 yen to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Henry Willmore, senior economist at Barclays Capital described the trading atmosphere as tenser and more nervous than usual, particularly as Asia's stock market losses knocked European bourses and the U.S. market significantly lower. \par \par \uc2  In late trading, the Dow Jones industrial average had pared some losses to a 1.35 percent drop. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless traders said Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, would not authorise dollar sales during a fierce dollar rally because that would simply allow traders to lengthen their dollar/yen positions at cheaper levels. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is going to grind higher against the yen because the situation in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very gloomy. To \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now would be to throw good money out the window and the U.S. is fully aware of that," said a dealer at a U.S. investment bank. \par \par \uc2  Traders also said there would be no element of surprise if the U.S. stepped in again near levels which seemed to trigger the June \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the U.S. sold dollars for yen in the open market for the first time in six years. \par \par \uc2  On June 16, the dollar hit a multiyear high at 146.75 but then slipped back toward 142 in early trading on June 17 when central banks caught the market off guard and lifted the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by more than seven yen during the U.S. day. \par \par \uc2  Washington may also be reluctant to help Tokyo now as Keizo Obuchi, the new prime minister, appears barely more aggressive in tackling Japan's bad debt than his predecessor Ryutaro Hashimoto who was turned from office because of slow reforms. \par \par \uc2  "The issues concerning Japanese reform progress and China's reported yen sales suggest that it would be foolish for the U.S. to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now," McCarthy said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials have been relatively critical of Japan's recent reform efforts and urged Japan to do more and faster. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. is not going to help them now as they have not really begun to help themselves," a dealer quipped. \par \par \uc2  Finally the Federal Reserve, which had earlier this year warned of the dangers of unbridled stock market gains, may actually welcome the softer yen's negative impact on the Dow, several dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The Fed may not be unhappy about wiping off the froth from the stock market's lips," McCarthy said, noting that slightly more modest U.S. growth may be welcome as it would allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push back the need for any rate hike. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand dealers and analysts agreed that any sort of disorderly trading conditions, either in the equity or \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, could force the Treasury to act, whether it is convenient or not. \par \par \uc2  Dealers warned if the dollar keeps rising at a clip of one yen or more for several days, Washington cannot stand by, and would probably \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth trading conditions and prevent markets from sensing they had gained the upper hand. \par \par \uc2  "If the (equity market is significantly disorderly, then we could see some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics. \par \par \uc2  ((-N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8b02xh8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
