\par\par \b \uc2  Japan PM repeats promises as yen battered further. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 1998\par 15:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 10 (Reuters) - With Japan's weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  putting pressure on other Asian policymakers, Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi admitted on Monday his economy was in a prolonged slump, but offered no fresh action to boost the economy. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi faced his first day of parliamentary questioning on his policies with the acknowledgement: "The economic slump is lingering and we are in a severe situation." \par \par \uc2  Although promising to do his utmost to pull the world's second-biggest economy out of recession through tax cuts and budgetary spending and to stabilise the banking sector, his prepared responses merely reiterated previous promises. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa told parliament the government was continuing to consider varying the rate of promised income tax cuts by income bracket to give special consideration to middle-income earners. \par \par \uc2  But while Obuchi was under pressure from the markets, where the yen fell near levels where \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June, the domestic political opposition proved unable to mount an immediate challenge to the government's financial system proposals. \par \par \uc2  The biggest opposition Democratic Party and two other groups cancelled a planned meeting to discuss a united front on legislation to deal with Japan's mountain of bad loans. Democratic officials said the party was waiting for the others, the Liberal Party and the Heiwa group, to finish their plans. \par \par \uc2  The Democrats have proposed their own versions of the bills, calling for immediate nationalisation of failed banks, which would then be run by banking specialists from the private sector with the aim of restructuring and re-privatising them by the end of March 2001. \par \par \uc2  Kansei Nakano, Obuchi's Democratic Party questioner in the Lower House session, slammed the government's bills to establish a "bridge bank" system to take over failed banks and take other measures to bolster the financial system. He accused the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) of continuing to protect banks with which it has close ties. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi merely repeated that he wants parliament to enact the bridge bank and other bills as soon as possible and that he would listen to the opposition and seek their cooperation. \par \par \uc2  The premier dodged Nakano's question about whether any of Japan's top 19 banks, specifically Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan Ltd, was now insolvent, saying only: "As of the March 1998 business reporting period, I understand that none of the top 19 banks had liabilities exceeding assets." \par \par \uc2  Nakano accused the government of hiding the amount of the nation's bad loans, suggesting the government had violated its principle of not saving insolvent banks when it used public funds to provide nearly two trillion yen in capital injections earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  Under questioning from another opposition member, Obuchi said it was "difficult at this point to predict whether a financial institution may fail." \par \par \uc2  But if the opposition failed to keep the government's feet firmly in the fire, the markets kept up the heat. \par \par \uc2  The dollar pushed to within a whisker of its high for the year of 146.75 yen set in June, largely boosted by news of yet another Japanese business failure, when unlisted copier maker Mita Industrial Co. Ltd filed for bankruptcy protection from creditors. \par \par \uc2  The top MOF bureaucrat, Vice Minister Koji Tanami, said of the yen's weakness: "We will deal appropriately and in a timely manner with excessive moves, and this policy has remained unchanged." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8a02uux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
