\par\par \b \uc2  Japan foreign bond purchases up sharply in July. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 1998\par 08:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 10 (Reuters) - Net purchases of foreign bonds by Japanese investors rose sharply in July compared with June, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) data showed on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Buying was attributed largely to continued weak domestic economic conditions as well as political uncertainty July, analysts and dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Certainly conditions were favourable for buying foreign bonds despite the dollar/yen rate," a senior analyst at a major city bank said. \par \par \uc2  Net foreign bond buying by Japanese investors, based on contracts, totalled 1.2335 trillion yen in July after net purchases of 507.5 billion yen a month earlier, MOF said. \par \par \uc2  Bonds include beneficiary certificates but exclude all bills. \par \par \uc2  Net buying by banks rose to 319.2 billion yen from 67.0 billion yen a month earlier. \par \par \uc2  Trust banks became net buyers with 183.9 billion yen in July after posting net sales of 23.9 billion yen in June. \par \par \uc2  "Some Japanese trust banks were detected actively taking profits after the U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-June," said a senior bond dealer at a U.S. brokerage house in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "They may turn into net sellers again if there is another joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But at the moment, the market does not seem too concerned over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks," he added. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, investment trusts may provide a constant flow of funds even after \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates become unfavourable, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese individuals will likely continue to invest in foreign bonds as long as a gap remains between U.S. and Japanese economic fundamentals, even if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates become adverse, another analyst said. \par \par \uc2  Investment trusts are becoming an increasingly relevant vehicle for foreign bond purchases by Japanese investors, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Interest in foreign bonds is extremely high, and now there is a pipeline (to funnel money) in the form of investment trusts," said Soichi Okuda, a senior capital markets economist at Nippon Credit Bank, Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Net buying by investment trusts stood at 89.6 billion yen in July, compared with 27.2 billion yen in June, according to MOF data. \par \par \uc2  "Interest has been heightened by financial institutions boosting their marketing efforts to woo the ordinary investor," since the so-called Big Bang financial deregulation was launched in April," Okuda added. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Japanese investors are shifting their assets to European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated instruments from U.S. Treasuries, analysts and dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Institutional investors are beginning to shift, ahead of the euro's launch" in January 1999, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  Toyoo Gyohten, former vice finance minister for international affairs, told Reuters in a recent interview that the avent of the euro may have significant implications for the dollar as it will give investors an alternative. \par \par \uc2  "People did not sell the dollar despite the huge debt held by the United States as there was nothing else to buy. But they may decide to buy the euro," Gyohten said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was buying of European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the German mark and ecu related to setting up of fresh investment trusts. \par \par \uc2  The mark has maintained the 82 yen level as Japanese investors remain eager to buy in connection with portfolio investments in European assets, dealers said. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8a02sk4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
