\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar just above lows in Europe on stocks woes. \b0\par\par\uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 1998\par 10:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 5 (Reuters) - The dollar was lower in European morning trade on Wednesday as concern about a slide in U.S. stocks dominated business, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A drop of nearly 300 points on Tuesday in the Dow Jones industrial average, its third worst point loss, pushed the dollar lower across the board. It weakened by two pfennigs and over one yen as Wall Street tumbled. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Dow's performance on Wednesday and further developments on Japan's tax reform plans would be the main factors affecting the dollar today. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei index closed little changed amid a rally in banking stocks and a flood of public pension buying. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is likely to track the stock markets today," said Simon Evans, senior dealer at Bank of Scotland. "It does seem amazing that the Nikkei is hanging on but the markets that look due for a correction are the FTSE and the Dow." \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose off overnight lows at 1.7658 marks and 143.40 yen to trade at 1.7684/94 and 143.85/95 at 0730 GMT, down from 1.7795/7805 and 144.73/83 in late Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen was little changed from Tuesday's late European levels after a strengthening yen pushed it to a low of 81.19 yen. The mark stood at 81.32/35 from 81.34/37 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's early European short-covering rally was hampered by offers just above 1.77 marks and 144 yen, dealers said. Flows were light, and traders did not expect significant movements ahead of the Wall Street open. \par \par \uc2  The Dow fell amid concern that corporate profits will remain weak in the second half of the year, hurt by Asian economic turmoil and a slowing domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  The risks for the dollar from a weak Wall Street is that a sell-off by international investors could weigh on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the outflow of capital. Historical data show a close correlation between the Dow and the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Whether or not we see further weakness in the dollar is a function of how the Dow performs this afternoon," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economist Europe at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "At this stage there is sufficient nervousness around that we may see some additional selling of the Dow this afternoon in which case ... there is some additional downside to the dollar," said Hawkins. \par \par \uc2  But analysts also said the downside risk for the dollar was limited because the majority of investors in U.S. stocks are domestics, while the Treasury market is more at the mercy of international investors. \par \par \uc2  Many European stock markets dropped sharply in early trade on Wednesday, with Britain's benchmark FTSE-100 index diving 2.3 percent at the open and Germany's DAX down over three percent. \par \par \uc2  The yen continued to receive support as the market eyed developments on tax cuts in Japan. The dollar has fallen from a high just above 146 yen on Tuesday to below 144 ahead of Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi's policy speech to parliament on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The market is more wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said Tuesday his remark last week that the yen was best left to markets was not meant to indicate there would be no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said on Wednesday Obuchi will tell parliament tax cuts will be "substantially over six trillion yen". \par \par \uc2  But traders said bearish forces are still looming over the yen. "What markets haven't read into the latest developments is that the tax cuts are not going to take effect tomorrow, or next month, but next year," said Evans at Bank of Scotland. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa also cited U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin as saying Asian economic woes were affecting the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Other officials kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  theme alive. Toyoo Gyohten, special adviser to the Japanese cabinet, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when necessary and when it will be effective. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's International Bureau, was quoted by Kyodo news agency as saying the yen's excessive weakness had yet to be corrected. \par \par \uc2  ((Astrid Zweynert London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du85012l6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
