\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asia markets retreat, eye jumpy yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stuart Grudgings \par \uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1998\par 15:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Reuters) - Most Asian markets sank Tuesday as traders eyed the volatile yen and shrugged off a new offensive by Tokyo warning of a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rescue. \par \par \uc2  The yen crumbled in early trade, falling past 146 to the dollar before rebounding later to 144/145, but renewed weakness helped drag Tokyo stocks nearly 1 percent lower. \par \par \uc2  Traders said moves on Tokyo's stock market mirrored the yen's rollercoaster ride. The Nikkei average ended down 141.50 points, or 0.88 percent, at 16,023.58. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong shares ended up after an early tumble, but shares in Jakarta, Manila and Thailand took heavy losses amid fears that the yen weakness could prompt another round of market turmoil. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment was also hit by weak U.S. stocks. The Dow Jones Index retreated further on Monday, ending down nearly 100 points or one percent. \par \par \uc2  After receiving a dismal welcome by the Japanese public and markets since its formation last Friday, the country's beleaguered new government went on the attack on Tuesday, warning financial markets to beware of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said markets had misinterpreted remarks he made Friday to mean market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unnecessary. \par \par \uc2  "My statements may have been insufficient or sent the wrong message," Miyazawa said. "It was not my intention to say that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unnecessary." \par \par \uc2  The last time the yen was this weak against the dollar was in mid-June, before the monetary authorities of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  But most markets remained sceptical over whether Japan's monetary authorities would back words with action and if the U.S. would be willing to join in again to help the yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen failed to take much heart from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threats, despite bouncing from a seven-week low of 146.05 hit in early trade. It recovered two yen but then slipped towards the 145 level later. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong shares clawed back big early losses caused by a poor set of bank results to end slightly higher. Traders put the sharp recovery down to short-covering in the futures market rather than a upturn in sentiment. \par \par \uc2  "The market's recovery in late trade is largely due to index futures and has nothing to do with fundamentals," said Tony Yung, research director at Tai Fook Securities Co Ltd., adding the Hang Seng could fall below 7,350 in the near future. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip Hang Seng Index rose 28.03 points, or 0.37 percent, to finish at 7,580.80 after recovering from a low of 7,377.81. \par \par \uc2  Traders in Jakarta said regional weakness had been the catalyst for a sharp fall in the share market's blue-chip stocks which saw the overall index slide nearly three percent. The composite index ended at 461.35, down 13.91 points. \par \par \uc2  New government steps to revive Thailand's recessionary economy sparked little enthusiasm from analysts or Bangkok markets. \par \par \uc2  The 25-point plan, including a big injection of funds into the agriculture sector and a raising of the public sector budget deficit, came with a prediction that the economy would show signs of recovery by early 1999. \par \par \uc2  But it failed to distract traders from the grim outlook for the Thai banking sector on Tuesday. The main SET index ended down 1.09 percent. \par \par \uc2  South Korean shares took a brighter view than most on the regional outlook, rising on hopes that the yen would be prevented from falling much further, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Seoul main index was up 333.20 points by the close, a gain of 1.13 percent. \par \par \uc2  On other markets, Manila shares slid two percent and Singapore's main index was down one percent by the close. Shares in Kuala Lumpur ended narrowly mixed. \par \par \uc2  Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly steady. The Indonesian rupiah held up at around 13,000 to the dollar, the Korean won moved up to 1,237 from 1,241, and the Malaysian ringgit hovered around 4.15. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du8400y46\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
