\par\par \b \uc2  Japanese ministers go on offensive to protect yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Brian Williams \par \uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1998\par 10:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 4 (Reuters) - Japan's beleaguered new government went on the offensive on Tuesday to protect the sagging yen, warning global financial markets to beware of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With polls showing the popularity of Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi's administration at a record low just four days into its life, his economic mandarins, including Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, sent a clear message that a weak yen was neither in the interests of Japan nor of the world. \par \par \uc2  On a day of wide fluctuations, with the dollar rising above the 146 yen level and then falling back below 145 yen in just a few hours, both Obuchi and Miyazawa scheduled meetings with U.S. ambassador Thomas Foley. \par \par \uc2  The mid-145 yen level was last seen in mid-June, before the monetary authorities of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said markets misinterpreted remarks he made last week to mean market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unnecessary. \par \par \uc2  "My statements may have been insufficient or sent the wrong message," Miyazawa said. "It was not my intention (last Friday) to say that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unnecessary." \par \par \uc2  "It is unavoidable for us as monetary authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets so that we can counter disruptive moves in the markets." \par \par \uc2  At separate news conferences Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya and Trade Minister Kaoru Yosano joined in the chorus. \par \par \uc2  Yosano said it was a matter of course for countries to conduct joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "when a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under speculative attack". \par \par \uc2  "It is of common interest for the world economy," he added. \par \par \uc2  Sakaiya said further yen weakness would not be favourable for Japan or the world because it would cause trade imbalances and possibly increase the flow of Japanese capital overseas. \par \par \uc2  "Markets often move excessively, but they eventually correct themselves. However, the current yen level is excessive," Sakaiya said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had hit a seven-week high of 145.89 yen in New York on Monday, in a reflection of market uncertainty over the ability of the new Japanese government to revive Tokyo's recession-hit economy. \par \par \uc2  Opinion polls published by Japanese newspapers on Tuesday showed low public support for Obuchi and his government. \par \par \uc2  A survey by the Mainichi Shimbun found 48 percent of respondents said they did not support the Obuchi cabinet - the highest disapproval rating for a new government since the newspaper began conducting such surveys in 1960. \par \par \uc2  A poll conducted by major financial daily Nihon Keizai Shimbun found 50.9 percent of respondents disapproved of Obuchi's government. \par \par \uc2  The Mainichi survey found just 25 percent said they supported the Obuchi government, while the Nikkei survey gave a support rating of 25.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Polls by the Asahi and Yomiuri Shimbun newspapers released earlier in the week also showed record high public disapproval ratings. \par \par \uc2  At his news conference Miyazawa had good news for consumers, saying changes to Japan's tax system now being discussed will result only in tax cuts and that the total could swell beyond the six trillion yen ($41.3 billion) that had been expected. \par \par \uc2  "At this stage, they must be tax cuts," he said. "As far as I'm concerned there will be no tax raises this time." \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said he would like to couple a cut in the top income tax rate, already promised by Obuchi, with across-the-board fixed-rate income tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du8400xa3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
