\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar easier in Europe after Japanese rhetoric. \b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1998\par 10:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 4 (Reuters) - The softer dollar was expected to remain capped in European morning trading on Tuesday after comments from Japanese officials fuelled fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit briefly topped a fresh seven-week high of 146 yen in Asian trading before falling almost two yen as Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa joined a chorus of officials in verbally defending the battered Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said he did not intend to say that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unnecessary when commenting last week that yen levels and stock prices would best be left to the markets, except in exceptional circumstances. \par \par \uc2  "What appeared to be a green light for selling yen is no longer so after this concerted effort to talk up the yen," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC Wood Gundy Oppenheimer. "The market is more cautious now." \par \par \uc2  Yen losses had accelerated after Miyazawa's comments last week amid market disappointment about the lack of reform action from the new government of Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  got a further battering on Monday amid signs the government would stop propping up asset markets. The benchmark Nikkei index continued weakness limited the dollar's downside after it fell to a low of around 144.20 overnight. \par \par \uc2  At 0735 GMT, the dollar was at 145.00/10 yen and 1.7804/14 marks, down from 145.66/68 and 1.7841/46 in late Europe Monday. Mark/yen was at 81.39/42, down from 81.61/64. \par \par \uc2  Traders remain skeptical about Japan's ability to make good on an ambitious reform package, including some six trillion yen in tax cuts. But the market is now expected to adopt a steady tone until Friday when Obuchi is expected to outline his plans in a speech to parliament. \par \par \uc2  "As anticipated the market is becoming a bit nervous about staying mega-short yen ahead of Friday's Obuchi announcement," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read. \par \par \uc2  Analysts doubted though that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was imminent since the pace of yen losses had slowed now. \par \par \uc2  The yen had fallen by about five yen per dollar since last Thursday, when Miyazawa made his initial remarks about \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  The sharp losses were seen as increasing the risk that it would revisit the near-eight-year low at 146.75 per dollar, hit in mid-June just prior to joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  Dealers will be watching out for further comments from Obuchi and Miyazawa, both scheduled to meet U.S. ambassador Thomas Foley on Tuesday for "courtesy calls." \par \par \uc2  A speech by U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers at 1430 GMT will also be eyed. \par \par \uc2  Urging Japan late on Monday to repair its banking system and stimulate demand, Summers said that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  did have its place but ultimately policy was most important. \par \par \uc2  He said U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin says the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it is appropriate, and not when it is not appropriate. \par \par \uc2  Some of the yen's overnight gains were attributed to options-related dollar and mark sales. A large amount of knock-out options are said to be located around 146.25-50 yen, and around 81.80-82.00 yen against the mark, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The data slate is fairly empty today, with only U.S. leading indicators for June due at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  ((Astrid Zweynert London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du8400swl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
