\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF pressured to alter  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  funding. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 1998\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being forced to review the way it raises funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , government sources say. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the ministry is reluctant to make changes in what is one of its principal sources of power, but will have to do so to succeed in its aim of boosting the yen's international status. \par \par \uc2  But they said while changes might bring the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  more in line with global standards, any shift without setting a strategic goal for the yen's value could make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movements even more volatile than now. \par \par \uc2  The ministry has been under pressure from the Bank of Japan (BOJ) and politicians to revamp the way it raises funds to use for dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The financial daily Nihon Keizai Shimbun said on Sunday that Japan would introduce price-competitive auctions for sales of financing bills (FBs) in fiscal 1999/2000, starting next April, to boost the yen's international status. \par \par \uc2  But senior MOF official Haruhiko Kuroda denied the report on Monday, saying: "We haven't made such a decision at all." \par \par \uc2  MOF raises funds for dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the issue of FBs, with interest rates set 0.125 percentage point below the discount rate. The discount rate is 0.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  Due to low yields, FBs are not always in great demand and the BOJ and other public bodies are obligated to buy whatever issues are left unsold. \par \par \uc2  Because of the current system, the MOF has had access to an unlimited amount of funds to use for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the system also prevents overseas investors from having access to yen-denominated assets, and for that reason is seen as a deterrent to global use of the yen. \par \par \uc2  For decades, the BOJ has said FBs should be sold in the market rather than having to be purchased by the BOJ, arguing that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  involvement in financing government debt by rolling over short-term debt could lead to inflation. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry, for its part, has strongly resisted the BOJ's calls, mainly for fear of losing flexibility in raising funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But there are signs of a change in the ministry's stance because of its desire to raise the yen's status and to compete with the advent of the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1999. \par \par \uc2  "We are aware of the issue in connection with the promotion of the yen's international use," said one government source. \par \par \uc2  In May, then-finance minister Hikaru Matsunaga floated the idea at a meeting of finance ministers from the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum of boosting the yen's status by deregulating yen asset holdings. \par \par \uc2  It was the first time a Japanese finance minister had advocated a boost in the yen's status, although a top ministry official was reluctant at that time to review the way FBs are sold. \par \par \uc2  A ministry source also said on Monday that reviewing FB sales is one of a number of options that need to be considered. \par \par \uc2  "Since the ministry has carried out various deregulation steps in the past, the regulations that remain are difficult to liberalise easily," he added. \par \par \uc2  "However, with the Finance Ministry losing power, it is becoming hard to dismiss outright calls for deregulation," he added. \par \par \uc2  Taisuke Tanaka, a strategist of Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Japan at Credit Suisse First Boston, said ending the current fund-raising method would make it more expensive for the MOF to amass funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and thus give it little reason to accept the change willingly. \par \par \uc2  "Moreover, the impact on the ministry would not be small as the current fund-raising method is a source of its power... It can raise funds as much as it wants without disclosing much information about it or being checked by others," he added. \par \par \uc2  Outstanding FBs amounted to about 31 trillion yen at the end of July. \par \par \uc2  Tanaka said if the current methods are changed, there is also a danger of making \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates even more volatile by encouraging a wider range of overseas players to hold the yen. \par \par \uc2  This makes it difficult for monetary authorities to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  under control, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Without building up a clear and solid strategy for the yen, securing its stability is seen as a long way off and other nations could take advantage of the situation for their own interests," he added. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8301rpc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
