\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Stocks Close 1% Lower As Yen, Bank Shares Plunge \b0\par\par\uc2 907 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 1998\par 08:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Tokyo stocks closed more than 1% lower Monday on growing investor skepticism that Japan's economy will quickly recover from its worst recession in decades. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, the blue-chip Nikkei 225 index fell 213.89 points, or 1.3%, to close at 16165.08. Friday, the Nikkei rose 177.37 points. Losers outnumbered winners 8 to 3 as an estimated 300 million shares changed hands on the first section, down from Friday's 379 million shares. \par \par \uc2  Share prices were hurt by the weakening of the yen against the dollar as well as futures-led selling. Major bank issues and high-technology stocks were among losers. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking was also attributed as a factor in Monday's retreat in the Nikkei, which had gained 2.8% over the four previous trading sessions on expectations that the new Japanese government would implement strong economic policies. \par \par \uc2  Trading remained light as many investors avoided making significant moves as they wait and see how Japan's new Prime Minister, Keizo Obuchi, will prop up the faltering economy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 145.13 yen, above 144.65 yen late Friday in New York. Earlier, the dollar hit a seven-week high of 145.13 yen, its highest level since trading at a more than eight-year high of 146.73 yen on June 16. \par \par \uc2  The dollar advanced despite some caution among traders that pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher too aggressively may invite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Also weighing on the dollar was another warning from China on Friday over the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness. \par \par \uc2  While Obuchi has promised $42 billion in income tax cuts and $69 billion in stimulus spending, analysts say a turnaround in Japan's economy could take time. Those concerns were reinforced Friday when Japan announced its jobless rate in June rose to 4.3%, its highest since the government began taking such statistics in 1953. \par \par \uc2  And signs are also appearing that Obuchi faces political difficulties. Two opinion polls conducted over the weekend show his public approval ratings to be among the lowest in recent decades for an incoming Japanese premier. Obuchi is to finalize his economic plans in a speech before Parliament later this week. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Nikkei bank-sector index was the third-largest loser Monday, falling 2.9% on expectations of accelerating industry realignment and after the emergence of negative news surrounding the sector. \par \par \uc2  The Financial Supervisory Agency said Friday it was imposing penalties on nine banks and four brokerage houses because their employees bribed officials of government agencies or state-affiliated institutions. \par \par \uc2  "The steep fall in financial shares damped sentiment," said Kunihiro Hatae, general manager at Tokyo Securities Co. "Until Obuchi announces the details of his policies, the market should move in range of 16000 to 16500." \par \par \uc2  Shares in Sanwa Bank tumbled 118 yen, or 9.6%, to 1,115 yen after the FSA banned the bank from the sale of mutual funds at its branches and from setting up a holding company for one year. \par \par \uc2  Selling also followed Standard & Poor's lowering of its credit ratings Friday on the Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan. LTCB shares briefly fell as low as 49 yen, and ended the session down 1 yen at 50 yen. \par \par \uc2  Among brokerage houses, Nomura Securities lost 22 yen to 1,663 yen, Daiwa Securities fell 6 yen to 623 yen, and Nikko Securities ended down 4 yen at 564 yen. \par \par \uc2  Shares of major chip makers and semiconductor-related companies were weaker after a newspaper report that semiconductor makers are likely to scale down capital investment this fiscal year through March 1999. NEC, Fujitsu, Mitsubishi Electric, Hitachi, Advantest, Tokyo Electron and Kyocera fell moderately to sharply. Only Toshiba fared well among the five biggest chip makers, rising 7 yen to 598 yen. Monday's Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported that Toshiba and auto-parts maker Yazaki Corp. will collaborate to develop traffic-control systems. \par \par \uc2  Among other blue chips, Sony and Bridgestone slid, while Fuji Photo managed to post gains. Honda Motor fell 100 yen to 5,290 yen, and Toyota was unchanged at 3,550 yen amid news that Japan's vehicle sales fell 8.4% in July, for the 16th-consecutive month of on-year declines. \par \par \uc2  Precision-machinery makers bucked the market's downward trend, becoming one of the only five sectors which posted gains. Minolta gained 13 yen to 950 yen, Olympus Optical was up 28 yen at 1,548 yen, and Nikon advanced 5 yen to 903 yen. \par \par \uc2  Canon rose 60 yen to 3,360 yen on a Nihon Keizai Shimbun report Saturday estimating record pretax profit in the first fiscal half ended June 1998. The newspaper said earnings would rise because of brisk demand for printers in the U.S. and Europe and the weaker yen. "Ahead of the fiscal half-year end in September, these leading precision-machinery makers are likely to revise their earnings estimates higher due to continued robust business from overseas markets," said Takeshi Kubota, general manager at Meiko Securities Co. \par \par \uc2  Iron trader Yahagi Corp. gained 33 yen, or 26%, to 158 yen, making it the top gaining stock. Yahagi shares had been heavily sold on rumors that the company faced financial difficulties over over-issued promissory notes. Yahagi officially denied the rumors Friday. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du83013ud\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
