\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's New Premier Aims To Improve Image As Economic Problems Mount \b0\par\par\uc2  By David P. Hamilton, Staff Reporter of The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 1,155 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 1998\par 06:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -- New Japanese Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi is launching an image campaign to convince the Japanese public, the financial community and Japan's trading partners that he is both determined and capable of fixing the economy and a serious banking crisis. \par \par \uc2  Yet it remains far from clear that Mr. Obuchi can succeed. Indeed, new challenges to his administration seem to pile up daily, while some members of his cabinet have stumbled visibly. \par \par \uc2  In his first news conference since being named prime minister on Thursday, Mr. Obuchi on Friday pledged to work for an economic recovery within two years and announced that he had appointed Toyoo Gyohten, a former vice minister at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as a special adviser on financial matters. Mr. Gyohten is a fixture at international conferences, who has close ties with financial officials in the U.S. and elsewhere. He will effectively become Mr. Obuchi's economic spokesman -- a kind of "pipeline," as Mr. Gyohten puts it, to explain the administration's economic policy to foreign governments and financial markets. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Obuchi said he also will soon convene an "economic strategy council" of as many as 10 businessmen and academics within a week. And in an unprecedented move, Mr. Obuchi also has named a press secretary specifically to deal with the foreign media, in a sign of the importance the administration attaches to getting its message out to an international audience. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Obuchi's outreach program speaks to the deep skepticism with which both financial markets and the Japanese public view the prime minister, an establishment figure within the ruling Liberal Democratic Party who has never held a major economic post. A poll by the daily newspaper, the Yomiuri Shimbun, found that Mr. Obuchi's government is the most unpopular new administration in nearly a decade, with a support rating of only 33%. A full 37% of respondents said Mr. Obuchi should resign immediately; 77% of those polled also thought the LDP had merely shrugged off its big electoral defeat in upper house elections just three weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Obuchi has tried hard to make a virtue of his lack of economic experience, joking in his Friday news conference that he is no "superman" who can solve all of Japan's problems by himself. Yet the new prime minister and his advisers will have to make some headway soon against a number of ominous economic developments. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, for instance, the government announced that rising bankruptcies and corporate cutbacks pushed up unemployment to a record high of 4.3% in June, up from 4.1% in May. The same day, the government reported that consumer prices in Tokyo -- an early indicator of nationwide trends -- fell 0.8% in June from the previous month, the largest such drop in eight years. Falling consumer prices could contribute to deflation in the country, which in turn could further damage production and boost unemployment. \par \par \uc2  A 16.65-trillion-yen ($115.1 billion) stimulus package passed earlier this year should start lifting the economy soon, helping Japan avoid the worst consequences of falling prices and weak demand. Yet few believe it will do much more than that; some economists, in fact, predict that the economy will contract this year by as much as 1% to 2% despite the package. That is particularly dangerous given that the government's top priority is to resolve Japan's banking crisis, in which its banks are burdened with more than $620 billion in problem loans. Any resolution of that problem is likely to cause additional economic pain in the form of unemployment and bankruptcies. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Obuchi on Friday promised quick action on his pledge to cut taxes by more than six trillion yen and to put together a fresh spending package valued at at least 10 trillion yen. New Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa told a Japanese newspaper that he is considering implementing the tax cut in two stages -- first as an unspecified general reduction of all income-tax rates, to be followed by a general revision of the entire tax system. Mr. Obuchi is expected to outline this approach in a policy speech on Aug. 7. \par \par \uc2  The sagging economy and a restive public aren't Mr. Obuchi's only challenges, though -- his own cabinet has been equally busy causing trouble. Mr. Miyazawa, for instance, caused a furor in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets late last week when he told reporters that "in extreme circumstances, there will be something like the past U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but such things shouldn't happen often." Traders took Mr. Miyazawa's comments as an indication that the government no longer favored a stronger yen, and sold off the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While Mr. Miyazawa quickly recovered and insisted that he meant only to articulate a "general principle" and not a change in policy, his departure from the kind of carefully scripted comments financial officials usually make hasn't helped the new government's image. What's more, the markets have continued to drive the yen down; late Friday in New York, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen to a level of 144.80 to the dollar, its lowest level in six weeks. \par \par \uc2  Matters for Mr. Obuchi weren't improved when his new agriculture minister, Shoichi Nakagawa, was immediately embroiled in controversy after telling a news conference that he didn't believe the Japanese military had forced Asian women to serve in brothels as so-called comfort women before and during World War II. Mr. Nakagawa later retracted his remarks. \par \par \uc2  Faced with such challenges and the need to win confidence overseas, small wonder that Mr. Obuchi has tapped a 45-year-old English speaking Foreign Ministry official, Akitaka Saiki, to serve as his personal spokesman to the foreign press. Mr. Saiki's appointment is groundbreaking for the prime minister's office, which until recently was so focused on domestic media that it even made a point of ejecting foreign reporters from one of former Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto's recent news conferences. \par \par \uc2  In his first meeting with foreign reporters on Friday, the urbane Mr. Saiki did his best to spiff up Mr. Obuchi's image, emphasizing the prime minister's determination to quickly address the economy's problems and describing the prime minister as an ox who, once aroused, "can run with amazing speed." Yet the urbane Mr. Saiki is still new at his job, and sometimes spun so quickly he risked losing his balance. At one point, for instance, he said Mr. Obuchi intends to quit once he has "revitalized" the economy, underscoring the prime minister's singular determination to fix the nation's finances. But a few moments later, Mr. Saiki reconsidered: "If the people think he should serve further, why not?" \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du83013si\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
