\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS - Yen sales may be met by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 1998\par 20:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 2 (Reuters) - When \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new finance minister last week said markets should move on their own, it sounded like an invitation to sell yen but analysts said the message also implied a free-fall will not be tolerated. \par \par \uc2  "The wording was implicit but explicitly it probably says Japan's 'save the yen at all costs' policy is out the door," explained JP Morgan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist James McCormick. \par \par \uc2  Coupled with Japan's weak economic fundamentals and banks' estimated $1 trillion in bad debt, Miyazawa's "realistic statements, coming out quite early in his tenure, signalled a screaming buy for dollar/yen," McCormick continued. \par \par \uc2  Traders took Miyazawa's message to heart and sent the yen tumbling two yen in a day and a half, letting it close at 144.60/63 yen to the dollar on Friday. Next, they appeared to lust for a dollar worth 150 yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts, however, warned it may take time to reach that goal as a quick-paced yen drop is sure to prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales, aimed at preventing another round of devaluations in Asia. \par \par \uc2  "If the yen falls another 2-1/2 yen in the next two days, the Federal Reserve will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," predicted Chris Widness, international economist at Chase Securities. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless dealers are ready to sell more yen. \par \par \uc2  "The market is interpreting this as a policy of benign neglect and the question is how quickly we will get to that 150 yen level" said Jeffrey Yu, senior trader at Sanwa Bank. \par \par \uc2  Feeding the markets appetite may be a sense the United States is will tolerate Japan's efforts at reflating its economy by letting the yen ease a bit, analysts speculated. \par \par \uc2  While President Bill Clinton on Friday urged banking reforms and fiscal stimulus, the White House also said it was encouraged by Miyazawa's early comments - a more detailed summary of policy intentions than many had expected. \par \par \uc2  Washington may also understand that Tokyo's options to steer the economy out of its worst downturn in decades are limited. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see how the United States could ask for a lot more than what is on the drawing board. Because Tokyo is doing the things Washington pressured it to do, there may be a little bit more give in letting the yen depreciate," said Henry Willmore, senior economist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  A Wall Street analyst who asked not to be named added, "There is a sense that there is some coordinated effort to let the yen go a little bit." \par \par \uc2  This perceived subtle shift in policy, both in Tokyo and Washington, may be possible now because the two nations managed to successfully de-couple the dollar/yen trade from global markets by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The 144.70 yen we see now as compared with the same levels seen before the (June 17) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a very different feel. There isn't a sense of urgency with markets around the world linked to the dollar/yen trade," McCormick said. \par \par \uc2  As both Miyazawa and U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers suggested there may be times \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are appropriate, the market seemed to realise central banks were on amber alert to stop dollar/yen from shooting through roof. \par \par \uc2  "The threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been wiped away," McCormick said. \par \par \uc2  If it were to happen, the Bank of Japan and the Federal Reserve Bank would probably act to "calm the nerves of other Asian nations," where a weaker yen could set back efforts to reignite growth, Willmore added. \par \par \uc2  While some traders said such an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be useless as it would merely offer more people to lengthen dollar/yen positions at cheaper levels, others disagreed. \par \par \uc2  "Traders would be very impressed to see the Fed again, after the last time they were in we had a definite correction," Widness concluded. \par \par \uc2  ((-N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1638 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8201miw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
