\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS-Yen vols spring up on Japan  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  comments. \b0\par\par\uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1998\par 14:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 31 (Reuters) - Implied volatilities in dollar/yen and mark/yen options rose in European trade on Friday as the yen lost ground across the board on comments made by Japan's new finance minister Kiichi Miyazawa. \par \par \uc2  The yen dropped to its weakest in six weeks against the dollar and a 5-1/2-year low against the mark, with yen bears emboldened by Miyazawa's comment - made at his inaugural news conference - that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock price levels should be left to the markets to decide. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa later clarified his comments, saying that he had been speaking in a general context and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary, but sentiment in the yen remained bearish. \par \par \uc2  Volatilities moved accordingly and rose across the curve. \par \par \uc2  "As far as the spot market is concerned, after Miyazawa's comments there's less wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so there's quite strong demand in dollar calls," said Yoshihisa Yamanaka, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  options manager at Sanwa Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "One-month vols tend to be sold off a little bit if spot dollar/yen shows signs of stalling, but they're still strong and risk reversals are favouring dollar calls," he added. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen volatilities were quoted at 15.00/15.50 percent at 1130 GMT, up from 14.45/14.85 seen in late European trade on Thursday. One-year vols were also higher at 15.80/16.30 percent against 15.60/16.00. \par \par \uc2  "Vols in the short end are obviously higher, but vols are firmer further out on the curve as well because the market had been rather short dollar calls at the long end," said one options trader at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Demand shifted further in favour of dollar calls, with 25-delta risk reversals at 0.25/0.55 compared with 0.05/0.20 in late European trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Spot dollar/yen had cooled off somewhat after its earlier rally, slipping back to 144.22/32 from its high of 144.50. But it was still 1.3 percent higher than levels seen at the close of European trade on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  One-week volatility in mark/yen were also higher but vols further out had barely changed, with traders citing some profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  One-week mark/yen vols had risen to 13.00/14.00 percent from Thursday's level of 12.50/13.50, while one-month stood unchanged at 13.10/13.60 against 13.10/13.40. \par \par \uc2  Spot mark/yen had rallied to 81.14/17 by Europe's midsession, its highest since October 1992 and up 0.7 percent from late Thursday's levels. \par \par \uc2  Volatilities in dollar/mark lost ground as spot recovered to return to more familiar territory after falling to an eight-week low on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/mark vols were at 7.60/8.00 percent against 8.20/8.50 percent late on Thursday. Demand for mark calls had slackened somewhat, with risk reversals at 0.35/0.65 against 0.4/0.7. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du7v018ak\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
