\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  when necessary-Miyazawa quoted. \b0\par\par\uc2 190 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1998\par 06:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 31 (Reuters) - New Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary, saying comments he made earlier in the day were just commonsense, Kyodo news agency reported. \par \par \uc2  "All I expressed was a commonsense view. It is obvious that we will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market when necessary," Kyodo quoted Miyazawa as telling reporters at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa earlier said he basically believed in leaving yen and stock prices to the markets. \par \par \uc2  "Put simply, the yen and stocks should be left to the markets. If there's an extreme situation, like the recent joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that's all right," Miyazawa told a news conference earlier on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose sharply in New York on Thursday on those comments, briefly rising as high as 143.85 yen from an opening level of 142.00/10 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was around 143.58/68 yen in early afternoon Tokyo trade, off its earlier high of 143.87 yen. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du7v030kw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
