\par\par \b \uc2  Miyazawa Says Yen Should Be Left To Market Forces, Not  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 961 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 1998\par 07:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa caught markets here off guard early Friday when he failed to deliver the same yen-supportive rhetoric as his predecessors. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa, speaking at his first press conference since being named to the post, said the yen should move on its own and that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets shouldn't occur often. \par \par \uc2  "Putting it very simply, whether it is the yen or stocks, we should rely on the markets to move on their own," Miyazawa said. "In extreme circumstances, there will be something like the past U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but such things shouldn't happen often," he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar reversed earlier weakness and rose sharply against the yen in New York in response to Miyazawa's comments. Late Thursday, the dollar was quoted at 143.72 yen, up from from 142.43 yen late Wednesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa's remarks didn't necessarily mean Japan wouldn't defend the yen. After all, U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin appeared to downplay the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-June in similar words, saying it "can be useful" but that "fundamentally over any period of time ... \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will follow fundamentals." A few days later, the U.S. joined \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, Miyazawa's comments represented a dramatic shift. Ministers in the last government repeatedly insisted that excessive yen weakness was "undesirable," that it would "correct" itself, and that Japan was ready "to take appropriate measures at the appropriate time" to combat yen weakness. \par \par \uc2  Just this week, outgoing finance minister Hikaru Matsunaga repeated the mantra, saying the yen wasn't at suitable levels and should strengthen. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa's comments seemed sure to fuel speculation that the new government, with the political freedom to make a fresh start on policy and with a greater recognition of the depth of Japan's economic problems, might be more willing to tolerate a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  In recent months Japanese officials and private economists have raised the idea of reviving the economy through massive injections of liquidity by the Bank of Japan, which would deliberately create a measure of inflation. Such a policy would almost certainly have to be accompanied by a weaker yen and would mark a dramatic policy shift for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose avowed goal is to aim for noninflationary growth. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa, who appeared at the press conference with new Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi, also said stimulating the nation's stagnant economy, not implementing fiscal reform to cut the budget deficit, will be the main priority of the new government. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi has said he plans to draft a 10-trillion-yen ($70 billion) package to stimulate the economy, although he hasn't said when he wants it to be adopted. Obuchi has also pledged six trillion yen in permanent corporate and income tax cuts next year. \par \par \uc2  For his part, Miyazawa would say only that the government will implement the 10-trillion-yen package if needed. He said details of the tax cuts need to be finalized as soon as possible, and that the government will have to issue deficit bonds to cover the cost of such cuts. Boosting consumption via tax cuts is the best way of stimulating the economy, Miyazawa said. \par \par \uc2  However, he said he didn't believe all of the 10 trillion yen in extra money mentioned by Obuchi will need to be spent if a solid recovery takes hold. He also said the government should put together its various economic plans before deciding on revisions to its fiscal-stringency law, which commits the government to certain deficit-cutting targets. Obuchi has said the law will be suspended. \par \par \uc2  On restructuring the nation's troubled banking industry, Miyazawa said he didn't believe any of Japan's 19 biggest banks will collapse during the process. He also said he didn't think the restructuring would be held up because of political turmoil and that he expects the banking industry's woes to be resolved within a year. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, as widely expected, the Japanese parliament on Thursday officially chose Obuchi as Japan's new prime minister. Obuchi received 268 votes out of 497 cast in the powerful lower house. The head of the largest opposition party, Naoto Kan of the Democratic Party of Japan, received 164 votes. Later, the upper house nominated Kan as prime minister, and he won 142 upper house votes compared to Obuchi's 103. \par \par \uc2  But since the lower house has the power to overrule the upper house, Obuchi was declared the winner after the two chambers failed to reach a consensus on the matter. It was the first time since 1989 that the chambers have split over who should be prime minister. \par \par \uc2  The 61-year-old Obuchi succeeds Ryutaro Hashimoto, who resigned after the ruling party's poor showing in parliamentary elections earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  The choice of Miyazawa as finance minister was a key issue facing Obuchi because the finance minister is expected to play a crucial role in cleaning up Japan's debt-ridden banking system. Obuchi's own lack of experience in financial affairs made Miyazawa's selection even more important. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Rubin urged Japanese officials to move "quickly' on banking and economic measures to stimulate growth and help address some of the critical problems facing the global economy. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. Treasury Secretary, speaking during an appearance on National Public Radio, offered something of a verbal olive branch, warning that Japanese officials' past problems in taking steps necessary to right the economy shouldn't be used as a basis for judging prospective actions under the Obuchi government. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du7u012on\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
