\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen slips on Japan govt concerns. \b0\par\par\uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1998\par 18:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 27 (Reuters) - The yen weakened to two-week lows against the dollar and five-week lows against the mark in Europe on Monday as concerns over fiscal reform in Japan grew. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the market was spooked by a report that ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) policy chief Yukihiko Ikeda said Japan needn't suspend a stringent budget-cutting law. \par \par \uc2  "That's got people wondering that if they won't revise the law are they really going to have the scope to come out and re-inflate the economy as the market wants to see them do," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economist for Europe at Bank of America. "The background for the yen is a bit more negative now." \par \par \uc2  At 1520 GMT dollar/yen was trading at 142.30/35 compared with 141.06/11 in late Europe on Friday and after hitting a session and two-week high of 142.50 in Europe. Mark/yen was trading at 79.77/80 compared with a session and five-week high of 79.95 and 79.34/37 in late European trading on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "It is not necessary to revise the Fiscal Reform Law in this extraordinary session," Ikeda was quoted as saying by Jiji Press ahead of an extraordinary parliament session starting Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister-designate Keizo Obuchi, who will be installed as premier at the session, has vowed to suspend the law, which hinders the government's ability to spur the flagging economy. \par \par \uc2  The law, already revised once to loosen its fiscal strictures, requires the government to cut the budget deficit to three percent of gross domestic product by March 2006 and cut issuance of deficit-financing bonds every year. \par \par \uc2  Economists also said dealers were transfixed by speculation over who will become Japan's next finance minister, with the financial markets favouring Seiroku Kajiyama, who lost to Obuchi in the LDP's presidential race. \par \par \uc2  "It's a sideshow, but it's definitely exercising peoples' minds," said Francis Breedon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers in London. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi is expected to officially name his cabinet when parliament resumes on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Two other options to head Japan's finance ministry are Koichi Kato, former secretary-general of the LDP and Kiichi Miyazawa, a former Prime Minister. Jiji reported on Monday that Obuchi telephoned Miyazawa to discuss the position. \par \par \uc2  Lehman said mark/yen made its gains based on growing speculation that the U.S. economy was slowing down. \par \par \uc2  "We're looking at the Q2 GDP and people are talking about a negative number and that's quite a big change for the U.S. economy," Lehman said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. second quarter advance real GDP is due out on Friday and a Reuters poll of economists on average forecast it to be flat compared with a rise of 5.4 percent in the previous period. \par \par \uc2  Economists said some speculation the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to arrest the yen's decline has been restraining it from rapidly declining after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said earlier that it would deal in timely and appropriate manner on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability. \par \par \uc2  In other news, despite the mark's strength against the yen it slid through 84 centimes against the Swiss franc on renewed concerns about Russian financial woes. \par \par \uc2  Russian markets took a fresh hammering on Monday after Russian Prime Minister Sergei Kiriyenko, fighting a deep financial crisis, told security chiefs that Russia's economic woes posed the biggest threat to national security. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, Finance Minister Mikhail Zadornov told Handelsblatt newspaper that Russian gross domestic product would shrink by up to one percent in 1998 and at best stagnate in 1999. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, June industrial production data are due out on Tuesday at 2350 GMT, June consumer prices and unemployment figures are due at 0030 GMT on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling markets were meanwhile looking ahead to the second quarter CBI industrial trends survey on Tuesday. The survey is expected to confirm recent signs of a slowdown in UK growth. \par \par \uc2  At 1630 GMT sterling was at 2.9547/57 marks and $1.6553/63 compared to 2.9495/2.9505 and $1.6598/$1.6608 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, paul.casciato@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7r0014z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
