\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr ends US up as Japan cabinet awaited, C$ routed. \b0\par\par\uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1998\par 22:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 27 (Reuters) - The dollar ended higher versus the yen on worries that Japan's new leadership might not implement economic reform quickly, while dollar/mark staged a late rally after mark/yen came off its highs, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The greenback also surpassed Canadian $1.50 for the first time ever even as traders grew edgy that the Bank of Canada would soon take action to contain the rise. \par \par \uc2  The yen was soft on fears that Keizo Obuchi, designated by the ruling Liberal Democratic Party on Friday to become Japan's next prime minister, might choose key ministry posts from the party old guard, rather than from reform-minded members. \par \par \uc2  "We'll probably just hang out and wait to see who Obuchi fills his cabinet up with," said Ben Strauss, a vice president at Bank Julius Baer and Co. "The early nominees so far don't look too promising and the market is voting thumbs down." \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen hit a five-week high at 79.95 in early U.S. trade. The dollar rose to 142.50 yen, closing at 142.42/45, up from 142.22/32 at the open. It ended near its high at 1.7863/73 marks up from 1.7800/10, playing catch-up late in the session as the cross retraced a bit. \par \par \uc2  Dollar shorts may also have covered as the Dow industrials reversed an 81 point loss, ending up 91 points, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Rumours that an Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was offering helped cap the dollar, as did talk of options-related sell orders at 143. \par \par \uc2  Overnight remarks by Japan's vice Finance Minister Koji Tanami that the ministry was keeping a close eye on both the dollar's rise above 142 and the 2.55 percent fall in the Nikkei 225 share index on Monday kept \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears alive. \par \par \uc2  Concerns that measures to lift \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of its deep recession may not get fresh impetus with the transition from Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto to Obuchi were underscored by talk that 78-year old Kiichi Miyazawa might be offered the key finance minister post. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto stepped down after his party suffered a disasterous loss of seats in parliamentary elections on July 12 amid voter anger over the economy. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa, a prime minister in the early 1990's, is seen as a symbol of bureaucratic inaction. Many investors associate his government with the onset of the long slowdown which Japan is still coping with. Most expect him to decline the job if offered. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Canadian dollar fell to a new record low at 1.5053 per U.S. dollar, continuing a string of almost uninterrupted losses this month. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a recovery in global commodity prices was key to restoring confidence in Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Canada is still heavily dependent on exporting natural resources. \par \par \uc2  Eric Nickerson, head global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America said that the rise in dollar/yen emboldened speculators to buy the dollar against the Canadian unit, especially because the Bank of Canada has so far done little to discourage them. \par \par \uc2  "We're sort of inclined to think we're getting to a level now where the Bank of Canada is going to have to do something to change attitude," said Nickerson, referring to a possible interest rate increase to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at C$1.5034/39, up from C$1.4985/90. \par \par \uc2  News that U.S. existing home sales fell 2.3 percent in June to 4.72 million units on an annualized basis had no impact on the market. The market awaits June durables goods data on Wednesday and the advance report on second quarter gross domestic product on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to 1.5035/38 Swiss from 1.4943/53, tripping stop-loss buy orders above 1.50, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The pound fell to $1.6570/80 from $1.6585/95. The Australian dollar slipped to $0.6085/90 from $0.6090/95. \par \par \uc2  ((-Alden Bentley, 212 859 1667)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7r000ml\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
