\par\par \b \uc2  Markets may let Obuchi prove himself - U.S. analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1998\par 19:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 27 (Reuters) - The man expected to become Japan's next prime minister has little time to shed his image as a cautious bureaucrat but financial markets may be patient a while longer, U.S. analysts and traders said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Three days after he comfortably became president of Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party, Foreign Minister Keizo Obuchi was being scrutinised by financial markets, eager to see whether he can implement reforms to guide Japan out of recession. \par \par \uc2  "The market is busy analysing the Obuchi win and who might be in his cabinet and what they will do," said Dennis Heidt, chief dealer at Paribas. Obuchi's choice for finance minister will be a key to the view of his administration, market players said. \par \par \uc2  While Obuchi's election had been widely expected and he had promised tax cuts while campaigning, market players generally disliked him as a bureaucrat who has successfully steered clear of scandals but also failed to excite anyone along the way. \par \par \uc2  "People are still very sceptical about the way Japan Inc. works and Obuchi is a part of that. Markets preferred (former chief cabinet secretary Seiroku) Kajiyama but because they picked Obuchi, dollar/yen is at these levels," said Steven Jonathan, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen opened New York trading at 142.22/32 after closing at 141.63/66 on Friday and dealers said it was significant to see the dollar at the levels where the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 17. \par \par \uc2  The market's general scepticism and desire to extend long dollar/yen positions however was kept in check by Obuchi's own promises and his ongoing efforts to pick a cabinet. \par \par \uc2  Over the weekend Obuchi travelled to Manila and pledged to U.S. Secretary of State Madeleine Albright that he would implement reforms while acknowledging that his reputation may force him to work even harder. \par \par \uc2  During the campaign, Obuchi said he would cut taxes by $43 billion and spend some $71 billion on a package of measures to stimulate the economy. \par \par \uc2  "In an odd way, the fact that Obuchi is seen as a bit of an underdog may actually speed up the reform process," said Henry Willmore, senior economist at Barclays Capital. He explained that Obuchi may feel more pressure to step up reforms than someone who the market had viewed as more reform-minded to begin with. \par \par \uc2  Besides Obuchi himself, the market is also rather interested to hear who will be appointed as finance minister. \par \par \uc2  "The market has the opinion that they could actually bring in a candidate who could make a difference," said Grant Wilson, a yen trader at Mellon Bank. \par \par \uc2  Former premier Kiichi Miyazawa and LDP heavyweight Koichi Kato are seen as the favoured candidates with Japanese newspapers speculating that Miyazawa will be offered the job but step aside to let Kato be appointed. \par \par \uc2  But until all appointments have been made and a new government is in place for U.S. officials to address, dealers predicted there may not be a huge appetite to sell yen. \par \par \uc2  "It is hard to say how forceful the market will want to get and there is a sense the market's frivolity about buying dollar/yen is not there" Wilson said, explaining dealers may be put off by fears of more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Merrill's Jonathan agreed that the "weapon of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still there and that Japanese and U.S. officials are talking to each other and making promises about reform. This could well keep trading a little subdued," he added. \par \par \uc2  But once the week is over and all candidates are picked, dealers left no doubt about their demands. "It does not really matter who is in there, but he has to take the right steps because otherwise patience will run out," Wilson said. \par \par \uc2  ((-N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1638 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7r0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
