\par\par \b \uc2  Yen steadies in Europe, eyes on Japan politics. \b0\par\par\uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 1998\par 12:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 13 (Reuters) - The yen was steadier against the dollar and mark at the European midsession, slightly stronger from overnight levels but still on a nervous footing amid the political uncertainty in Japan. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said yen trade would continue to be volatile as the market weighs the impact of Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto's resignation on Japan's efforts to reinvigorate its economy. \par \par \uc2  "The yen reaction has been quite encouraging given what's happened," said Nick Stamenkovic, analyst at Bank Austria Creditanstalt in London. "The market is beginning to anticipate more fiscal stimulus on the horizon." \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto's resignation after his ruling Liberal Democratic Party's poor showing in weekend elections pushed the yen to an overnight low of 144.28 per dollar. It recovered some ground from those lows alongside firmer Japanese asset markets and by 1050 GMT it stood at 142.45/55. \par \par \uc2  Japanese markets found some support on the perception that Hashimoto's successor would have to adopt a more expansive policy, especially after the Japanese electorate's waning support for the LDP. \par \par \uc2  But analysts remained cautious that such hopes will materialise into new stimulus measures. \par \par \uc2  "It's difficult to see how anyone can have enough confidence to be buying yen at current levels," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Paribas in London, speaking on Reuters Television. "I think those 'bottom-picking' in Japanese markets right now will be sadly disappointed." \par \par \uc2  As well as the political uncertainty, Japan's gloomy financial outlook still remains to keep the pressure on the yen. \par \par \uc2  Also working against the yen is the perception that further declines in the Japanese unit are unlikely to be supported by coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's political and economic policy outlook becomes clearer. \par \par \uc2  Until then, most analysts see the yen losing more ground against the dollar and Paribas' Parsons sees levels up to 150 yen in this quarter. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark, meantime, was also steady after its overnight declines. As well as finding pressure from gains in mark/yen, dollar/mark was being weighed down by optimism that Russia and the IMF had agreed a deal to avert a financial crisis in Russia. \par \par \uc2  Reports of such a deal, said to be worth around $12.5 billion, lifted the mark, which the market traditionally sees as the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  most exposed to Russian turmoil. The IMF and Russia are expected to make a joint statement at 1300 GMT today. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been expecting some kind of IMF agreement with Russia but the country still has some serious problems. This news may only prove to be a short-term relief for Russian markets," said Bank Austria Creditanstalt's Stamenkovic. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 1.8105/08 marks, up from an overnight low around 1.80 marks but still shy of late Friday's European level of 1.8180/90 marks. \par \par \uc2  ((Andrew Reierson, London newsroom +44 171 542 6745, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7d012hp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
