\par\par \b \uc2  FEATURE- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  policy seen key to euro/dlr balance. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 1,133 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 1998\par 11:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 6 (Reuters) - What if the euro keeps rising after its launch, leaving the dollar far behind? \par \par \uc2  Such a scenario is quietly feared in Washington, and how the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) copes if it comes true will be the first test of the new bank's credibility and the prospects for the euro, Japanese officials and analysts say. \par \par \uc2  WHO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENES\b0 \highlight\uc2  IF MASSIVE CAPITAL SHIFT TO EURO? \par \par \uc2  A growing market consensus says the euro will strengthen early in its life, prompted by a global flow of capital into Europe and away from the U.S. dollar, traders say. \par \par \uc2  Former U.S. presidential economic advisor Fred Bergsten has repeatedly said the euro will almost certainly become a major international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , boosted by substantial portfolio diversification even in its early years. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Bank of New York President William McDonough said last month: "We've heard that some investors have already begun to switch some of their portfolios from a dollar-denominated one into either a mark-or ECU-denominated one." \par \par \uc2  The key question is which of the two - the ECB or the US Federal Reserve - would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to moderate a euro rise if world capital kept favouring Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the ECB would be naive enough to check the strength of its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Masao Suzaki, professor of economics at Tokyo's Senshu University. \par \par \uc2  The ECB, in line with global \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market practice, would be likely to tell the U.S. Treasury and the Fed that the nation with the falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should act to defend it, Suzaki said. \par \par \uc2  ECB EXPECTED TO INHERIT BUBA'S POLICY \par \par \uc2  The market is also betting the ECB will inherit the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of the Bundesbank, which is seen as regarding the strength of the mark as a symbol of its power. \par \par \uc2  "If the ECB justified a euro selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the simple argument that a cheap dollar could cause recession in Europe, it would, in fact, spook the markets," said Takeshi Hanai, executive manager of the Industrial Bank of Japan's International Treasury Dept. \par \par \uc2  A strong euro would contain inflation, while undermining the global competitiveness of products made by EMU members. \par \par \uc2  But ECB President Wim Duisenberg said at his confirmation hearing at the European Parliament in May that the future impact of exchange rate changes on the European economy would be much less significant than it is at present. \par \par \uc2  Trade with countries outside the EMU was seen as accounting for only around 10 percent of total trade by member countries, he said. \par \par \uc2  The ECB also has the right to refuse to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, defying any calls to do so from politicians, if such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would risk stoking inflation. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is like a huge aircraft carrier, as opposed to a group of destroyers. Any quick, nervous moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are quite unlikely," Hanai of IBJ said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 'S CRITICAL MISTAKE IN \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  POLICY \par \par \uc2  When it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) offers a lesson in what not to do, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "The worst policy mistake \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities have ever made in their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  history was let themselves be responsible for defending the dollar when it fell relentlessly in 1995," Suzaki said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a post-war low of 79.75 yen and fell to 1.3450 marks in mid-1995. The BOJ then bought massive amounts of dollars for yen to stop the U.S \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's freefall. \par \par \uc2  "There was no justification for Tokyo to defend the dollar at its own cost when the huge U.S. current account deficit was then a major factor in the dollar's weakness," Suzaki said. \par \par \uc2  This role reversal between Tokyo and Washington was one reason the BOJ had struggled to retain market credibility as it tried to defend the falling yen in recent months, he added. \par \par \uc2  It was only when the United States joined forces to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar hit an eight-year high of 146.75 in June that the yen recouped some of its dramatic losses. \par \par \uc2  U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  WAR CHEST MAY NOT BE ENOUGH \par \par \uc2  Yet analysts point out that the United States may not have a big enough war chest of foreign reserves to defend the dollar in the event the euro overwhelms the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve Bank of New York said the Federal Reserve System had $11.48 billion worth of marks in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the start of 1998, while the U.S. Treasury Exchange Stabilisation Fund had $5.81 billion worth of marks. \par \par \uc2  U.S. external reserves of $70.33 billion are ranked only the world's seventh largest, after those of Singapore and Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  "The next time a dollar crisis hits, the dollar will be forced to give up its status as a key \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said one fund manager at a private asset management firm. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the reason the United States - the world's largest debtor - had so far avoided the kind of debt crisis suffered by Asia and Latin America was because foreigners were willing to hold on to debt in the world's leading \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan has said the cost to Washington of funding U.S. government debt is reduced by $10 billion to $15 billion each year because foreigners are willing to hold large amounts of dollar debt. \par \par \uc2  U.S. DEBTS THREATEN DOLLAR'S GLOBAL STATUS \par \par \uc2  Former presidential advisor Bergsten has forecast the net debtor position of the United States, which has already passed $1 trillion, will shortly exceed $1.5 trillion. \par \par \uc2  In its annual report released in June, the Bank for International Settlements said: "the BIS is concerned that the markets will lose patience with the accumulation of U.S. external debt and drive the dollar sharply lower." \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the launch of the euro may trigger a reversal in the dollar's current strength to reflect huge U.S. debts. \par \par \uc2  But it could also spur the United States into taking action on its economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "The advent of the euro could motivate the United States to tighten its fiscal discipline and to refrain from depending so much on foreign capital," a senior BOJ official told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Kosuke Nakahira, an advisor to the Institute for International Economic Studies, said the U.S. current account deficit was unlikely to dethrone the dollar from its key \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  position overnight. \par \par \uc2  But the euro would certainly lighten the global responsibility the United States currently bore, a burden perhaps heavier than was warrented by the nation's economic power, said Nakahira, a former Japanese vice finance minister for international affairs. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk (813) 3432-8676 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7801ffy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
