\par\par \b \uc2  US players fear dlr/yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  but not today. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1998\par 17:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 2 (Reuters) - As markets review Japan's plans to reform its banking system and look ahead to a three-day U.S. holiday weekend, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers were on high alert for any signs of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But while nervousness hangs over trading floors, many dealers said they expected no action on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "I think they will wait until later, they won't act today," said Henry Willmore, senior economist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that central banks may buy more yen has peaked and ebbed several times since the Federal Reserve Bank of New York and the Bank of Japan spent billions to stabilise the yen on June 17 and it reached new heights this week, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  All along \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said central banks would probably time any new attack to coincide with news on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reforms. Since the June \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts speculated Washington may have agreed to assist Tokyo in return for promises of far-reaching reforms. \par \par \uc2  To many, official dollar/yen sales on Thursday or Friday would make sense as central banks could hit the markets hard with many U.S. traders heading home early for the Fourth of July holiday, which will be officially observed on July 3. \par \par \uc2  Trading in Chicago's IMM \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures will wrap up at 1300 EDT/1700 GMT Thursday and resume on Monday. Many U.S. commercial banks will be thinly staffed on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve Board will be closed on Friday but the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, which handles all open market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will be open on Friday, a spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  As dollar/yen has been confined to a narrow range since opening New York trading at 140.60/70, dealers said central banks may be inclined to stay on the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  "The markets aren't doing anything really interesting," Willmore said. \par \par \uc2  "I think the Fed and Bank of Japan are more than happy to see two-way interest, and I think that alone would keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at bay, added John McCarthy, senior vice president at ING Barings Capital. \par \par \uc2  But traders admitted the pair was confined to its range precisely because \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears had trebled after the dollar zipped up nearly three full yen since New York's close on Wednesday and was now teetering around the psychologically key 140 yen to the dollar level. \par \par \uc2  The dollar shot higher as dealers were disappointed that Japan's plan for a bridge bank - which would take over the assets of failed banks - offered few new details. \par \par \uc2  "Any time we have a reversal to the upside after news that is supposedly yen supportive, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as we saw in April after the stimulus package was announced, is a concern," said Ben Strauss, a vice president at Bank Julius Baer. \par \par \uc2  Yet if central banks do not act today, analysts said investors may well express more disappointment with Japan's lack of forceful action and hit the yen again, possibly sending it to the next key resistance level at 142.25 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Analysts pinpointed two possible flashpoints for new dollar sales - around Japan's July 12 parliamentary elections and after U.S. President Bill Clinton returns from China. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the U.S. may well try to give Japan a little more time to sort out its plans, noting that miracles cannot occur overnight and that Tokyo deserves a fair amount of time to present its new plans. \par \par \uc2  Others said if China had indeed played a key role in convincing the U.S. to prop up the yen, then more promises to prevent the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling may have been made during Clinton's trip. \par \par \uc2  ((-NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7201m2o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
