\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen loses more ground in late trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1998\par 16:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 2 (Reuters) - The yen lost more ground to the dollar and mark into the end of the European session, finding little inspiration in Japan's plans to tackle its bad loans problem that were announced today. \par \par \uc2  The dollar pushed above resistance at 141 yen into late trade, gaining over two yen from the levels it stood at when the government announched its "bridge bank" plans in morning trade. \par \par \uc2  "The market got a bit overexcited about the prospect that today's plan would accompany an announcement on tax cuts, but what we saw was in line with expectations," said Michael Metcalfe, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at NatWest Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government's plan will put failed Japanese banks under public administration before dissolution while public bridge banks take over their healthy loan business. The intention is to wind up failed banks without hurting their healthy borrowers. \par \par \uc2  Today's announcement was unchanged from a draft plan made available overnight, disappointing a market hoping the government would beef up the plan with further measures. \par \par \uc2  At 1500 GMT, dollar/yen stood at 141.11/21, just off an earlier top of 141.35 yen but above pre-announcement levels around 139 yen and 138.63/68 seen late Wednesday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen stood at a session high of 77.50/53, up over a yen from early levels and well above late Wednesday's 76.32/35 yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that while today's plan can go some way to repair its financial system woes, more still needs to be done. \par \par \uc2  "There is still a lot of doubts over the funding of the (bridge bank) plan and how it will be implemented," said Nick Stamenkovic, chief European economist at Bank Austria Creditanstalt Futures in London. \par \par \uc2  "Japan is under a lot of international pressure and what will be key for dollar/yen direction is the U.S. reaction to the Japan plan." \par \par \uc2  Despite the market's disappointment with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bank plans, heightened wariness over Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen made dealers reluctant to push dollar/yen much higher. Friday's U.S. holiday was also keeping players sidelined and markets are expected to start thinning out later today. \par \par \uc2  "The economic backdrop is still very weak and with the bridge bank plan out of the way, the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is about the only thing supporting the yen," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "But with expectations so high for some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we probably won't even get a sniff of it." \par \par \uc2  There was little net reaction to today's U.S. non-farm payrolls data for June which came in just above average expectations. June payrolls grew by 205,000 after a revised increase of 309,000 in May and compared with 200,000 forecast. \par \par \uc2  Key was softer earnings growth which analysts said confirmed the perception of a slowing U.S. economy and an easing inflation threat. \par \par \uc2  "It shows the Fed was justified in keeping rates on hold at this last FOMC," said BACA's Stamenkovic. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was trading at 1.8198/03 marks, off an earlier 2-1/2 month high of 1.8260 and above Wednesday's 1.8170/80. \par \par \uc2  The mark, already under pressure by the financial turmoil in Russia, saw support eroded by comments earlier today from Bundesbank council member Hans-Juergen Koebnick. \par \par \uc2  He said he did not seen any reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates in the short-term and did not know whether a rise would by necessary by year-end. \par \par \uc2  ((Andrew Reierson, London Newsroom +44 171 542 6745)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7201lrb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
