\par\par \b \uc2  LDP bank plan will aim to reassure - ex-MOF official. \b0\par\par\uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 1998\par 13:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 1 (Reuters) - A plan to clear up bad loans held by Japan's banks and set to be unveiled by the ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) will aim to allay international fears over the nation's banking sector, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official says. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign nations likely will be reassured by the plan," Tomomitsu Oba, a former vice minister of finance for international affairs, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The LDP plan will likely be modeled after a U.S.-style bridge bank set up in the 1980s to resolve the U.S. savings and loan crisis, Oba said. \par \par \uc2  "I think it will be a good plan that will work positively toward the disposal of non-performing assets," he said. \par \par \uc2  Oba said the bridge bank would be "undoubtedly an extraordinary, emergency measure", and should be disbanded as quickly as possible. \par \par \uc2  He added that if the scheme is conducted properly and if the domestic economy picks up, most of the public funds poured into the banks will be recoverable. \par \par \uc2  The timeframe for a bridge bank would depend on the size and scope of bad loans carried by the financial institutions, which will be determined through inspections by the Financial Supervisory Agency, he said. \par \par \uc2  However, on the bridge bank scheme itself, Oba, who is president of the quasi-governmental Japan Center for International Finance, said he held reservations about putting too much emphasis on the U.S. system used in the 1980s. \par \par \uc2  "Japan should develop an unique system based on both the U.S. style and the Scandinavian approach," he said. \par \par \uc2  Oba said that transforming banks into public entities, such as Norway and Sweden did in the early 1990s, was more appropriate, considering the size of Japan's ailing banks and the amount of public funds to be earmarked for the scheme. \par \par \uc2  He said the Scandinavian model was initially aimed at large banks, whereas the U.S. solution in the 1980s targeted smaller institutions. \par \par \uc2  Under the U.S. approach being mulled, a bridge bank would temporarily put failed banks under public supervision to ensure healthy borrowers are not left in the lurch. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said on Wednesday that Tokyo needs effective action on its banks problems to help the yen recover. \par \par \uc2  Japan and the United States conducted joint dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on June 17 to prop up the yen. \par \par \uc2  Deputy U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided an opportunity for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet its promises on economic and bank sector reform, but that its impact would not last forever. \par \par \uc2  ((Tamawa Kadoya, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8785 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7101rso\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
