\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis At A Glance: Yen Up On Rubin, Tax Cut Hopes \b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 1998\par 11:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- U.S. Treasury Secretary Rubin denied saying the dollar would go to Y150 or any other level, noting that economic fundamentals should guide exchange rates and the U.S. will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when appropriate. He repeated the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  banking reforms. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government and LDP will enact a permanent Y2 trillion-Y4 trillion residential and income tax cut in 1999, Yomiuri reported. It said Prime Minister Hashimoto will announce the tax cut before Parliamentary elections July 12. A top LDP member called the report "false." \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell against the yen in the wake of a Y4.5 yen plunge Tuesday, on Rubin's comments and the tax cut report. At 0900 GMT, the dollar traded at Y138.63, down from Y138.93 late Tuesday in New York. The Nikkei rose 3.4%. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are higher against the dollar, following the yen's and Nikkei's rise. South Korea's stock index ended 5.9% higher. Singapore's index was 2.3% higher on the stronger local dollar. [*R/SK] [*R/SN] \par \par \uc2  -- South Korea's trade surplus widened to $3.92 billion in June from $104 million a year ago. The commerce ministry cited a 36% decline in imports. [*R/SK] \par \par \uc2  -- The Korean Financial Supervisory Commission said Kangwon Bank, Chungbuk Bank and Peace Bank of Korea are unlikely to meet the Bank of International Settlement's 8% capital adequacy ratio requirement by June 2000. Banks failing to meet the ratio may be closed or merged. [*R/SK] \par \par \uc2  -- Chinese President Jiang repeated China's commitment to a stable yuan and support for the Hong Kong dollar peg, and praised Chief Executive Tung for his measures to protect the territory from the Asian economic turmoil. [*R/CH] [*R/HK] \par \par \uc2  -- Thai consumer prices rose 10.7% in June, the biggest increase in a decade, but below IMF's 12.2% forecast. [*R/TH] \par \par \uc2  -- Moody's will review for possible downgrade ratings for Japanese trading companies Mitsubishi, Mitsui and Sumitomo, citing the adverse operating environment in Asia. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Indonesian President Habibie fired 41 Assembly members appointed by former President Suharto in a bid to rid the government of nepotism and corruption, but left Suharto's children in Parliament, a local newspaper reported. [*R/IO] \par \par \uc2  -- Bank Indonesia Governor Sjahril reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't lower interest rates until the rupiah strengthens. However, high interest rates haven't helped the rupiah, which is trading at IDR14,700 to the dollar. [*R/IO] \par \par \uc2  -- Some 3,000 Indonesian textile workers demanding higher pay protested in Java for the third day, police said, as the government announced a 15% minimum wage increase. [*R/IO] \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore Deputy Prime Minister Lee said it will be five years before high growth returns to Singapore, due to Asia's economic crisis, a Hong Kong newspaper said. [*R/SN] \par \par \uc2  -- The economic crisis in Southeast Asia has produced an unexpected peace dividend, slowing the buying of new weapons in the region, Australia's outgoing defense chief said. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 01-07-98 \par \par \uc2  0912GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du7105n91\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
