\par\par \b \uc2  Japan speeds up plan for bridge bank blueprint. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Kobayashi \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 1998\par 07:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 30 (Reuters) - Japan's plans for reorganising its banking sector picked up speed on Tuesday, as pressure from the United States and other countries continued amid fears that Japan's banking woes could trigger a global recession. \par \par \uc2  A plan to wind up failed banks without bankrupting healthy borrowers will be finalised on Thursday, a joint panel of the government and the ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) on bad loans announced on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  That is six days earlier than an original deadline of July 8. \par \par \uc2  Japan is mulling a U.S.-style "bridge bank" that would temporarily put failed banks under public supervision to ensure healthy borrowers are not left in the lurch. The United States used a bridge bank system to keep sound borrowers afloat in solving the savings-and-loan crisis of the 1980s. \par \par \uc2  "I want you to consider fundamentally introducing the functions of a U.S.-style bridge bank to create a system that can put a bank under public supervision immediately upon its failure," Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto told a meeting of key government and LDP officials on Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  The government - which has recently acknowledged that the estimated 77 trillion yen ($553 billion) in problem loans weighing on the banking sector is a key impediment to economic recovery - is scurrying to show leadership in solving the crisis before parliamentary elections less than two weeks away. \par \par \uc2  Markets as well as trade partners are watching carefully. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said in a speech to students in Bangkok on Tuesday that Japan's economic strength was key to recovery in crisis-hit Thailand and beyond. \par \par \uc2  "The focus of the world now is on Japan effectively addressing its banking issues in a manner that gains the confidence of the markets," Rubin said. \par \par \uc2  Peru's President Alberto Fujimori, in Tokyo for a two-day symposium on Latin America, told Reuters that it was important for Asia and Latin America that Japan overcome its financial problems. \par \par \uc2  Markets were cheered by hopes that the government may take drastic steps to clean up the financial sector's bad-loan mess, with the Nikkei stock average surging over three percent to end at 15,830.27 and the dollar sinking below 140 yen in late trading. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Ambassador to Japan Thomas Foley cautiously welcomed proposals to establish a "bridge bank". \par \par \uc2  "We are encouraged by the proposals we now see," he said, adding that joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets earlier this month opened a window of opportunity to take bold new action. \par \par \uc2  LDP officials have said that the plan for a bridge bank will be based on basic principles laid out by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), such as depositor protection, and a proposal from Parliamentary Vice Finance Minister Yasuhisa Shiozaki. \par \par \uc2  The Shiozaki plan calls for establishing a single bridge bank, under the jurisdiction of the semi-governmental Deposit Insurance Corp (DIC), which would take over all the assets of a failed institution, then transfer deposits, debentures and healthy loans to a successor bank. Bad loans would go to the existing Resolution and Collection Bank. \par \par \uc2  MOF, in a meeting on Sunday with the LDP, said the bridge bank should be formed as a public institution that would draw on 30 trillion yen ($215 billion) already earmarked to stabilise Japan's financial system. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto on Tuesday told top policy officials that if no private receiver bank emerges to take over a failed institution, the government should mull establishing a bridge bank to protect sound borrowers by using the DIC. \par \par \uc2  "We received various instructions from the prime minister. We hope to discuss the matter among the government and the party and finalise plans in a few days," LDP policy chief Taku Yamasaki told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto said the government would continue to urge financial institutions to disclose their bad loans and their restructuring plans, saying the bridge bank must have a strict inspection function. ($1=139) ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6u01wdb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
