\par\par \b \uc2  Early Tokyo: Dlr Below Y143 On Profit-Taking After Tankan \b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 1998\par 02:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell back below Y143.00 early Monday in the Asian session on profit-taking following the release of the Bank of Japan's "tankan" quarterly survey of business sentiment, after it rose above the level earlier for the first time since the June 17 U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint dollar selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 0023 GMT (8:23 p.m. EDT Sunday), the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded at Y142.55, down from around Y143.30 immediately before the release of the data, but up from Y141.98 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  "A weak tankan had been almost completely factored into trading before the release of the data," said Minoru Shioiri, senior trader at Nippon Credit Bank. "The data was within the range of market expectations, and Japanese investors sold dollars to book profits." \par \par \uc2  The data released by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed that diffusion index for Japanese big manufacturers dropped to minus 38 in June from minus 31 in the previous survey released April 2. The index shows the percentage of companies seeing conditions improving minus those saying conditions are worsening. \par \par \uc2  Economists surveyed by Dow Jones had forecast on average that the diffusion index for big manufacturers would fall to minus 44 in June. \par \par \uc2  Prior to the release of the data, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose as high as Y143.30 at 2324 GMT Sunday on the idea that the dollar would strengthen this week after news last Friday of an expected merger between two Japanese banks failed to stem the weak yen trend. \par \par \uc2  "Overseas players didn't attach significance to the merger news. (That fact) has revived the dollar-bullish sentiment," said a trader at a Japanese city bank. \par \par \uc2  Sumitomo Trust & Banking Co. and the Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan Ltd., known as LTCB, said Friday that they had agreed to aim for a merger in a year. LTCB had been looking for a merger partner to get itself out of a business slump due to massive problem loans. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also trading at DEM1.8100, lower than DEM1.8115 earlier and unchanged from DEM1.8100 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at DEM1.7985 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 29-06-98 \par \par \uc2  0031GMT \par \par \uc2  1074 1863 4162 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6t05hra\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
