\par\par \b \uc2  Rubin backs China's resolve to hold firm on yuan. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 845 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 1998\par 11:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, June 28 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin left China on Sunday to embark on a whirlwind tour of crisis-torn Asia, convinced that Beijing has the resolve to live up to its new image as an island of stability in the battered region. \par \par \uc2  During his three-day stay in Beijing on the sidelines of U.S. President Bill Clinton's state visit, Rubin received his strongest assurances yet that China will keep its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable to help prevent a renewed Asian financial thunderstorm. \par \par \uc2  China's "unambiguous" promise not to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came as an obvious relief to Rubin since bolstering Beijing's resolve to hold the line on the yuan has become a top priority on Washington's agenda in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Both Clinton and Rubin heaped praise on China for resisting the pressure to devalue in the face of financial turmoil, lauding Beijing's "statemanship" and forward-looking economic policies - even as they admitted it was in China's own interest to do so. \par \par \uc2  A devaluation would cut the price of Chinese products in terms of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making it easier for the country's exporters - the backbone of China's economy - to compete with other nations whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been weakened. \par \par \uc2  But China, in the midst of a historic economic transformation that has already brought about profound changes in the every day lives of China's 1.2 billion people, knows there is no such thing as a free lunch. \par \par \uc2  Not only could a devaluation in China set off a chain-reaction of similar moves by other Asian countries, thereby neutralising the effect of Beijing's move, China would also have to pay a steep political price, effectively gambling the respect it has gained in the West for resisting the temptation of a quick-fix devaluation. \par \par \uc2  "One continues to be impressed by the vision expressed by their leaders and by the understanding they express of the issues they face," Rubin told reporters accompanying him aboard an Air Force jetliner en route to Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury chief last visited China nine months ago. \par \par \uc2  Asked to compare his impressions now to what he had heard back then, Rubin said Beijing's commitment to overhauling its creaking economy and slowly opening it up to the outside world appeared to remain strong. \par \par \uc2  "They continue to express a determination to move along at a good pace," he said. \par \par \uc2  Still, the summit brought no breakthrough on Beijing's 10-year long effort to join the World Trade Organisation, which the United States continues to stall because it believes China needs to do more to its vast domestic market to foreign competition. \par \par \uc2  The ringing endorsement of Beijing's monetary stance was in stark contrast to the continued pleading with the region's erstwhile powerhouse, Japan, to finally do something - anything - to get its sickly economy back on its feet. \par \par \uc2  Japan is mired in a deep recession and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen, has fallen drastically against the dollar, making it harder for the rest of Asia to recover from its own difficulties. But years of urging Tokyo to finally tackle its economic weakness so far have fallen on deaf ears. \par \par \uc2  The imminent threat of a Chinese devaluation aimed at catching up with the plunging yen was a key factor behind the surprise U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  move to bolster the yen by jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market two weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  Even though the move had only a short-lived effect on the yen, which previously had hit its lowest level against the dollar in eight years, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong made a point of thanking Rubin for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Clearly, we have a fair bit of discourse with the Chinese about the region," Rubin said on Sunday. "The role of Japan is enormously important. It's integrally related to what's happening in Asia." \par \par \uc2  Rubin, who on Friday also met Finance Minister Xian Huaicheng and Prime Minister Zhu Rongji, the architect of China's reforms, has not ruled out the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the yen. \par \par \uc2  But Clinton on Sunday lamented the lack of a magic "wand" to make Asia's crisis go away, and agreed with Rubin that the key to the yen's stability was Japan's own economic policies. \par \par \uc2  "We can be supportive, but they have to make the right decisions," he said. \par \par \uc2  After a series of high-level meetings in Kuala Lumpur, Rubin was due to travel to Bangkok on Monday and continue on to Seoul on Tuesday in a whistlestop tour of the region designed to take Asia's economic pulse almost a year after its financial woes began. \par \par \uc2  Thailand and South Korea are among the hardest-hit of the former Asian tiger economies. Together with Indonesia, they are at the receiving end of bail-out deals totalling more than $120 billion, drawn up by the International Monetary Fund and conditional on harsh economic reform programmes. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6s021ie\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\c